hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5446","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik, al-Hasan, his brother Sa'id, and Jabir ibn Zayd Abu al-Sha'tha praying on Eid day before the imam comes out."
"5447","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: The Duha prayer is like the Fitr prayer, two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs."
"5448","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from a man, he said: People of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us on Eid day before the imam came out and prayed, and Ibn Umar came and did not pray. The man said to Ibn Umar: People of the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and prayed, and you came and did not pray. Ibn Umar said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, does not reject goodness to a servant - I think he said."
"5449","From Ibn al-Taymi, from a shaykh of Basrah, he said: I heard al-Ala ibn Zayd saying: Ali went out on an Eid day and found the people praying before he came out, so it was said to him: Would that you forbade them. He said: I am not one to forbid a servant when he prays, but I will tell you what we witnessed - or he said - what we attended."
"5450","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Mas'ud and Hudhayfah used to forbid the people - or he said - make sit whoever they saw praying before the imam comes out on Eid day."
"5451","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Alqamah ibn Qays was asked about prayer before the imam comes out on Eid day, and he said: The companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray before it. The questioner said: What if I have prayed? He said: I have told you of the practice of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and you know best."
"5452","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I went out with him on an Eid day, and he did not pray before it nor after it. He said: Then I went out, I and Masruq and Shurayh, to the open prayer ground, and we did not pray before it nor after it. Isma'il said: And a man stood praying on Eid day after the prayer, so Amir forbade him and did not let him pray after it."
"5453","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me that Sa'id ibn Jubayr would not pray before the imam comes out."
"5454","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me that the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) comes out."
"5455","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he would not pray before the two Eids nor after them anything. From Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar likewise, and he added, he said: He would not pray that day until the day turned. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn Umar likewise. From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar likewise, and he added, he said: He would pray the dawn prayer on Eid day in his clothes, then go early to the prayer ground."
"5456","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: We know of no one who prayed before the imam comes out on Eid day nor after him. From a shaykh of Ta'if called Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb used to command us not to pray before it nor after it."
"5457","From Ibn al-Taymi and others, from Shu'bah, he said: Adi ibn Thabit reported to us that he heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrating from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a Fitr or Adha day and prayed two rak'ahs, not praying before them nor after them."
"5458","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Ayyash informed me that Anas ibn Malik informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray before the Fitr prayer nor after it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray before the Adha prayer nor after it anything."
"5459","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: He would not pray before the two Eids anything, and would pray after them four."
"5460","From al-Thawri, from Salih, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Ibn Mas'ud would pray after the two Eids four."
"5461","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and Qatadah, that Ibn Mas'ud would pray after them four rak'ahs or eight, and would not pray before them."
"5462","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I saw Amir praying after the two Eids two rak'ahs."
"5463","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Has anything reached you of a prayer glorified with after the Fitr prayer? He said: No. I said: Except with what you increase is dearer to you? He said: Yes."
"5464","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from a freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, he said: He does not pray before them nor after them. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I saw Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar not praying before them nor after them."
"5465","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A narration was narrated raised to al-Sha'bi that he heard the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No prayer before al-Adha nor after it, nor before the Fitr prayer nor after it, until the sun declines."
"5466","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from a man he named, he said: We went out with Ali ibn Abi Talib on an Eid day to the open ground, and he saw people praying before the imam's prayer, so he said as though amazed: Do you not see these praying? We said: Will you not forbid them? He said: I dislike being like one who forbids a servant when he prays. He said: Then he began with the prayer before the sermon, and did not pray before it nor after it."
"5467","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me from Ibn Abbas and from Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, they said: There was no adhan on Fitr day nor on Adha day."
"5468","Then I asked him after a while about that, and he informed me, he said: Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari informed me that there is no adhan for the prayer on Fitr day when the imam comes out, nor after he comes out, nor iqamah, nor call, nor anything. He said: And no call that day, nor iqamah."
"5469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Abbas sent to Ibn al-Zubayr at the first of when allegiance was given to him that there was no adhan for the prayer on Fitr day, so do not call the adhan for it. He said: So Ibn al-Zubayr did not call the adhan for it that day. And he sent to him with that: The sermon is only after the prayer, and that was done. He said: So Ibn al-Zubayr prayed before the sermon, and his companions asked him, Ibn Safwan and companions of his said: Why did you not inform us? So the prayer escaped them that day. Then when what was between him and Ibn Abbas soured, Ibn al-Zubayr did not return to Ibn Abbas's instruction."
"5470","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that he witnessed the Eid with Umar, Uthman and Ali, and all of them prayed without adhan or iqamah."
"5471","From Isra'il, from Simak, he said: It reached me that he witnessed al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah on an Eid day, and he prayed with them before the sermon without adhan or iqamah, then he came being led upon his camel until he delivered the sermon after the prayer upon his camel."
"5472","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me from Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, he said: I heard him saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on Fitr day and prayed, beginning with the prayer before the sermon, then he addressed the people. When the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he descended and came to the women and reminded them while leaning upon Bilal, Bilal spreading his garment into which the women cast charity. I said to Ata: The zakat of Fitr day? He said: No, but it was charity they gave then; a woman would cast her toe-ring, and they kept casting and casting. He said: I said to Ata: Do you hold it due upon the imam now to come to the women when he finishes and remind them? He said: Yes, by my life, that is due upon them, and why do they not do that?"
"5473","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I witnessed the prayer on Fitr day with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman; all of them prayed it before the sermon, then delivered the sermon after. He said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and it is as though I look at him when he sits the men with his hand, then he came cleaving through them until he came to the women with Bilal; he said: O Prophet, when the believing women come to you pledging to you that they associate nothing with Allah - he recited this verse until he finished it, then said when he finished it: Are you upon that? One woman said, and none other of them answered him: Yes, O Prophet of Allah - Hasan does not know who she was. He said: Then give charity. He said: So Bilal spread his garment, then said: Come, may my father and mother be ransom for you. They began casting the toe-rings and finger-rings into Bilal's garment. We said to him: What is al-fatakh? He said: Rings of bone worn in the Jahiliyyah."
"5474","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray on Eid day, then deliver the sermon, then he thought he had not made the women hear, so he came to them and admonished them and said: Give charity. He said: A woman began casting the ring and the earring and the thing, then he commanded Bilal and he put it in a garment until he carried it away."
"5475","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh informed me that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrating: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out on Eid day and Fitr day and pray those two rak'ahs, then give the salam, then stand facing the people while they sat around him, saying: Give charity, give charity. The most who gave charity were the women, with the ring and the earring and the thing. If the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a need to dispatch an expedition against the people, he mentioned it, otherwise he left."
"5476","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Al-Harith ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Dhubab informed me from Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would begin on Fitr and Adha day with the prayer before the sermon, then deliver the sermon, and in his sermon would be the command of dispatch and of the detachment."
"5477","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd, the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that he witnessed the Eid with Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he prayed before delivering the sermon without adhan or iqamah, then delivered the sermon and said: O people, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting these two days: as for one of them, your day of breaking fast from your fasting and your Eid; and as for the other, your day in which you eat your sacrifices."
"5478","He said: Then I witnessed it with Uthman, and that was on Friday; he prayed before delivering the sermon without adhan or iqamah, then addressed the people and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting these two days: as for one of them, your day of breaking fast from your fasting and your Eid; and as for the other, a day in which you eat your sacrifices."
"5479","He said: Then I witnessed it with Uthman, and that was on Friday; he prayed before delivering the sermon without adhan or iqamah, then addressed the people and said: O people, this is a day on which two Eids have gathered for you; whoever of you is of the people of al-Awali, we have permitted him, so let him return, and whoever wishes, let him witness the prayer."
"5480","He said: Then I witnessed it with Ali, and he prayed before delivering the sermon without adhan or iqamah, then delivered the sermon and said: O people, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating your sacrifices after three nights, so do not eat them after that."
"5481","From Isra'il, from Simak ibn Harb, that he witnessed al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah on an Eid day praying without adhan or iqamah, then his camel was brought led to him until he delivered the sermon after the prayer upon his camel."
"5482","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam informed me that he witnessed al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah praying before the sermon, then he mounted a camel of his and addressed them; when he finished, he urged it on."
"5483","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from a man, he said: I witnessed with Abu Bakr on an Eid day, and he began with the prayer before the sermon without adhan or iqamah; then I witnessed it with Umar, and he began with the prayer before the sermon without adhan or iqamah; then I witnessed it with Uthman, and he began with the prayer before the sermon without adhan or iqamah."
"5484","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May a person remember Allah while the imam is delivering the sermon on Arafah day or Fitr day while he comprehends the imam's speech? He said: No; every Eid, no speech therein."
"5485","From Hasan ibn Umarah, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Silence is in four places: al-Jumu'ah, the two Eids, and istisqa."
"5486","From Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Listening attentively is obligatory in four places: al-Jumu'ah, al-Fitr, al-Adha, and istisqa."
"5487","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you know the first who delivered the sermon on Fitr day then prayed? He said: I do not know; I found the people upon that."
"5488","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me, he said: Yusuf ibn Abdullah ibn Salam informed me, he said: The first who began with the sermon before the prayer on Fitr day was Umar ibn al-Khattab, when he saw the people slipping away; after he prayed, he detained them in the sermon. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Yusuf likewise, except he said: Uthman ibn Affan."
"5489","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: The first who began with the sermon before the prayer was Mu'awiyah."
"5490","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me that the first who delivered the sermon was Mu'awiyah in the Eid - or Uthman at the end of his caliphate - Ma'mar doubted. He said: And it also reached me that Uthman did that, as his absentees would not catch the prayer, so he began with the sermon until the people gathered."
"5491","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh narrated to me that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: I went out with Marwan on a Fitr or Adha Eid day, he between me and Abu Mas'ud, until we reached the prayer ground, and Kathir ibn al-Salt al-Kindi had built for Marwan a minbar of brick and clay, so Marwan turned to the minbar until he was beside it, so I pulled him to begin with the prayer. He said: O Abu Sa'id, what you know has been abandoned. He said: No, by the Lord of the easts and the wests, three times, you bring nothing better than what we know. Then he began with the sermon."
"5492","From al-Thawri, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: The first who put the sermon before the prayer on Eid day was Marwan, so a man stood to him and said: O Marwan, you have opposed the Sunnah. Marwan said: O so-and-so, what was there has been abandoned. Abu Sa'id said: As for this, he has done what was upon him. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever of you sees an evil, if he can change it with his hand let him do so; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart, and that is the weakest of faith."
"5493","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When would those of old leave their houses on Fitr day for the prayer? He said: They would go out until the forenoon extended, then pray, then deliver a short sermon a little while, minimizing their sermon? He said: They would not detain the people at all. He said: Then they would descend and the people would leave? He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never sat upon a minbar until he died; he would only deliver the sermon standing. So how would it be feared they detain the people? They would only deliver the sermon standing, not sitting. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, one of them would ascend the minbar and stand as he is, standing, not sitting upon the minbar until he ascends it, nor sitting upon it after he descends. Their whole sermon was standing; they would only testify once, the first. He said: There was no minbar except the minbar of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until Mu'awiyah came with a minbar when he performed Hajj and left it. He said: So they kept delivering sermons upon minbars after."
"5494","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Abu al-Huwayrith, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Amr ibn Hazm when he sent him to Najran: Delay al-Fitr, remind the people, and hasten al-Adha."
"5495","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the sermon of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Friday was twice, standing. Ma'mar said: I said: Did that reach you from a trustworthy one? He said: Yes, whatever you wish."
"5496","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The people used to deliver the sermon on Friday twice, between them a sitting."
"5497","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he delivered the sermon would lean upon a palm trunk of the pillars of the mosque; when the minbar was made and he sat upon it, that pillar shook like the moaning of a she-camel until the people of the mosque heard it, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended and embraced it, and it fell silent."
"5498","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Aban narrated to me that Anas ibn Malik informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on Fitr day and Adha day would deliver the sermon upon his mount after the prayer. He said: He would testify, then recite a surah of the Qur'an, supplicate with supplications, then go."
"5499","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, that he witnessed al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah on an Eid day praying without adhan or iqamah, then his camel was brought led to him until he delivered the sermon after the prayer upon his camel."
"5500","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam informed us that he witnessed al-Mughirah praying therein before the sermon, then he mounted a camel of his, then addressed them; when he finished, he urged it on."
"5501","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Janab, he said: I heard Yazid ibn al-Bara ibn Azib narrating from his father, he said: When it was Adha day, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to al-Baqi', and a bow was handed to him, so he delivered the sermon upon it."
"5502","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I saw Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr delivering the sermon with a staff in his hand."
"5503","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have a minbar brought out, nor his companions, on an Eid day, and the first who brought out the minbar was Marwan. A man said to him: You brought out the minbar though it was not brought out, and you began with the sermon before the prayer though it was not done, and you sat in the sermon though they would not sit. He said: That Sunnah has been abandoned."
"5504","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out with him on Fitr day with a short spear, and he would plant it in the prayer ground before him and pray toward it."
"5505","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard one of the people of Madinah mentioning that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he delivered the sermon would lean upon his staff, leaning."
"5506","From al-Thawri, from a companion of his, from a man who narrated to him, from Ali, he said: I saw him coming to the Eid walking."
"5507","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, he said: Ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote urging them in the two Eids: Whoever can come to them walking, let him do so."
"5508","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, that he disliked riding on the Eid and al-Jumu'ah."
"5509","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Mihnaf ibn Sulaym, and he had companionship, he said: Going out on Fitr day equals an umrah, and going out on Adha day equals a Hajj."
"5510","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Of the Sunnah is that you come to the prayer ground on Eid day walking."
"5511","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade going out with weapons on the day of Eid. From Hushaym, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak the same, and he added therein: except if they fear an enemy, then they may go out."
"5512","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: When we have finished the prayer, whoever wishes let him wait for the sermon, and whoever wishes let him go. He said: So Ata used to say: Attendance at the sermon on that day is not obligatory upon the people."
"5513","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, he said: I heard that the takbir in Eid is nine and seven."
"5514","From Ma'mar, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud, that he said: The imam pronounces takbir on the day of Fitr, before he preaches, nine when he is about to stand, and seven in... I pressed him to explain to me better than this but he could not, so I thought his statement 'when he is about to stand' refers to the last sermon."
"5515","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, he said: The Sunnah is takbir upon the minbar on the day of Eid; he begins his first sermon with nine takbirs before he preaches, and he begins the last one with seven. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah similar to it."
"5516","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, that he heard Makhul saying: Between every two takbirs there is salat upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5517","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The takbir in the prayer on the day of Fitr is thirteen takbirs; he pronounces them while standing: seven in the first rak'ah, among them the opening takbir of the prayer, and among them the takbir for bowing, and among them six before the recitation, and among them one after it; and in the other six takbirs: among them one takbir for bowing, and among them five before the recitation, and one after it. I said to him: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that Ibn al-Zubayr used to pronounce only four in each rak'ah alike, pronouncing them in each two rak'ahs; we heard that from him. So Ata said: The one from whom I took this hadith is, and Allah knows best, greater than Ibn al-Zubayr. I said: Who? He said: Ibn Abbas."
"5518","From Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taifi, that he heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrating, from his father, from his grandfather: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pronounced seven takbirs on the day of Fitr in the first rak'ah, then recited, then pronounced the takbir for bowing, then pronounced five in the other, then recited, then pronounced takbir, then bowed."
"5519","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: Ali used to pronounce takbir in Adha and Fitr and Istisqa seven in the first and five in the other, and he would pray before the sermon and recite aloud. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman used to do that."
"5520","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from al-Harith, from Abu Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Kinana, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, I think he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he used to pronounce takbir in Adha and Fitr seven in the first and five in the last."
"5521","From Malik, from Nafi', he said: I attended Eid with Abu Hurayrah; he pronounced seven takbirs in the first and five in the last before the recitation. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Abu Hurayrah the same. From Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Abu Hurayrah the same."
"5522","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I heard him saying: The takbir on the day of Eid before the recitation is seven and five."
"5523","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sabrah, from Rabi'ah, and Abi al-Zinad, and Abdullah ibn Muhammad, and others: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pronounce takbir on the day of Fitr and Adha and Istisqa, one takbir, seven in the first and five in the other."
"5524","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Mas'ud: In the first five takbirs including the takbir for bowing and the opening takbir, and in the other rak'ah four including the takbir for bowing."
"5525","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, and al-Aswad ibn Yazid: That Ibn Mas'ud used to pronounce takbir in the two Eids nine nine: four before the recitation, then he pronounced takbir and bowed, and in the second he recites, and when he finished he pronounced four takbirs, then bowed."
"5526","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, and al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: Ibn Mas'ud was sitting, and with him were Hudhayfah and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, so Sa'id ibn al-As asked them about the takbir in the prayer on the day of Fitr and Adha, and each one kept saying: Ask this one, and this one saying: Ask this one, so Hudhayfah said to him: Ask this one, meaning Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, so he asked him, and Ibn Mas'ud said: He pronounces four takbirs, then recites, then pronounces takbir and bows, then stands in the second and recites, then pronounces four takbirs after the recitation."
"5527","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he mentioned that Ziyad asked Masruq about the imam's takbir; he said: The imam pronounces one takbir then pronounces four, then recites, then pronounces takbir, then prostrates, then stands in the last and recites, then pronounces three takbirs, then pronounces one with which he bows. Qatadah said: And it reached me the same from Jabir ibn Abdullah."
"5528","He said: Isma'il ibn Abi al-Walid informed us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, he said: I attended Ibn Abbas pronouncing nine takbirs in the Eid prayer at Basra, keeping the two recitations consecutive. He said: And I attended al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah doing that also, so I asked Khalid how Ibn Abbas did? So he explained to us just as Ibn Mas'ud did in the hadith of Ma'mar and al-Thawri from Abu Ishaq exactly."
"5529","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: On Adha on that day is there a fixed Sunnah upon the people of the horizons in anything they do? He said: One prayer like Fitr, and it is obligatory only in its congregation, two rak'ahs only, and slaughter if he wishes. And he said: It is due upon them to attend it, just as attendance at the Fitr prayer is due upon them."
"5530","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa: That in Adha, with them, there is the same takbir as there is with them in Fitr."
"5531","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa: That in Adha with them there is what is in Fitr."
"5532","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said to me: I do not dislike in the two rak'ahs of takbir on the day of Adha the same as on the day of Fitr, and that has not reached me from anyone."
"5533","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The takbir on the day of Eid in the first rak'ah is four, and in the last three, so the takbir is seven besides the takbir of the prayer."
"5534","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim said: The Sunnah of Adha is the Sunnah of Fitr except the slaughter. He said: And it is the same in going out and sermon and takbir except the slaughter."
"5535","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: The imam stands and pronounces takbir for the opening of the prayer, then pauses a while supplicating and remembering to himself, without any known statement having reached them, neither supplication nor otherwise, then pronounces the second takbir, then pauses likewise a while supplicating to himself and pronouncing takbir, then likewise between every two takbirs a while supplicating and remembering to himself until he pronounces six including the opening takbir, then he recites, and when he finishes he pronounces the seventh takbir for bowing, then he stands in the second, and when he stands upright, he pronounces takbir, then pauses a while supplicating to himself and remembering, then pronounces the second, then likewise until he pronounces five before the recitation, and when he finishes he pronounces the sixth, so those are thirteen takbirs, all of which the imam pronounces while standing. He said that more than once, and the takbir of prostration is not counted in that."
"5536","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Alqamah, and al-Aswad, from Ibn Mas'ud: That between every two takbirs is the measure of a word."
"5537","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there any tahlil or tasbih or hamd to be said on that day as the takbir is said, so that it is right to act upon it in the prayer, or after it, or before it, or upon the minbar? He said: It has not reached me."
"5538","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Does the imam raise his hands each time he pronounces this additional takbir in the Fitr prayer? He said: Yes, and the people also raise."
"5539","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali: Regarding the recitation in the two Eids, you let him who is next to you hear."
"5540","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, that he heard Tawus saying: There used to be recited in the prayer on the day of Fitr: Iqtarabat al-Sa'ah. He said: And I know only that he mentioned it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5541","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the prayer on the day of Eid: Qaf, and Iqtarabat al-Sa'ah."
"5542","From Malik, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Damrah ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard Ubaydullah ibn Utbah saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab went out on an Eid day, and asked Abu Waqid al-Laythi: With what did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in the prayer on the day of Eid? He said: With Qaf, and Iqtarabat."
"5543","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the prayer on the day of Eid: Sabbihi isma rabbik al-A'la, and Hal ataka."
"5544","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two Eids in the first rak'ah: the Fatihat al-Kitab, and Sabbihi isma rabbik al-A'la, and in the other: the Fatihat al-Kitab, and Hal ataka hadith al-Ghashiyah."
"5545","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite on Friday and in the two Eids: Sabbihi isma rabbik al-A'la, and Hal ataka hadith al-Ghashiyah."
"5546","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: It is from the Sunnah to come to the prayer on the day of Eid."
"5547","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is the prayer of the day of Fitr obligatory upon all the people? He said: No, except in congregation. He said: Friday is not more deserving of being attended than it in that, except in congregation, so how about Fitr? Ata said: They are not completed as four neither in congregation nor otherwise. He said: I said to Ata: Is it due upon the people of the village to attend the Fitr prayer, as attendance on Friday is due upon them? He said: Yes. He said: That repeatedly, and he had said to me another time before this: That is due, but not like the right of Friday; they were commanded with Friday. Then he said: There is no day greater than Friday; it is the greatest of all days, greater than the day of Arafah and the day of Fitr, and it has reached us that there is nothing, neither land nor sea nor tree nor stone, but it keeps supplicating on that day until the sun rises, except the two weighty beings, the jinn and mankind."
"5548","From Ma'mar, he said: I did not see Friday but more obligatory in their view than Fitr; they say: This is faridah, and this is sunnah."
"5549","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and Qatadah, they said: The Adha prayer is like the Fitr prayer, two rak'ahs two rak'ahs."
"5550","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I prayed the Fitr prayer without wudu, then remembered after the imam finished? He said: You repeat it. And Amr ibn Dinar said that to me."
"5551","From al-Thawri, from Bakr, from Ibrahim, he said: If you fear in the two Eids that the prayer will escape you while you are withholding urine, then urinate then perform tayammum."
"5552","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Abdullah said: Whoever misses the two Eids, let him pray four."
"5553","From al-Thawri: Regarding a man who misses a rak'ah of Eid, he said: He prays with the imam, then makes up the rak'ah he missed, and pronounces takbir as the imam pronounces takbir, and if he finds the imam reciting, he pronounces takbir as the imam pronounces takbir."
"5554","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ibrahim, he said: Whoever misses the Eid prayer with the imam, there is no takbir upon him."
"5555","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever misses the prayer on the day of Fitr prays as the imam prays. Ma'mar said: If the sermon or the prayer on a day of Fitr or Adha escapes a person, then he comes after that, he prays two rak'ahs."
"5556","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to distant villages, Fadak and Yanbu' and similar villages at a distance of three from Madinah, that they gather and pray the two Eids."
"5557","From Ibn al-Taymi, and others, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, he said: Abu Iyad and Mujahid were in hiding in the time of al-Hajjaj, and it was a day of Fitr, so Abu Iyad was spoken to, and he supplicated for them and led them in two rak'ahs. He said: And Shu'bah informed us, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah the freedman of Ibn Abbas, that he used to say the same."
"5558","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: There is no Jumu'ah nor tashriq except in a comprehensive city. Ma'mar said: By tashriq he means the day of Fitr and Adha: going out to the open ground."
"5559","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no Adha prayer nor Fitr prayer upon the traveller unless he is in a city or village, then he attends the prayer with them."
"5560","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, that a woman narrated to her, she said: My husband campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twelve campaigns, and I went out with him in five of them; we used to attend the sick and treat the wounded, and we were commanded in the two Eids that whoever among women has no jilbab, her companion should lend her from her jilbab. Hafsah said: Then Umm Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah came to us, and I mentioned that to her, and she said: Yes, may my father and mother be his ransom, he commanded us to bring out in the two Eids the unmarried maidens and those behind veils and the menstruating women. She said: As for the menstruating women, they keep away from the prayer place and witness the good and the supplication of the Muslims. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Hafsah bint Sirin the same."
"5561","From Abdullah, from Sa'id, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: The wife of Alqamah was a dignified woman, and she used to go out in the two Eids."
"5562","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi': That he would not bring out his womenfolk in Eid."
"5563","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: If Friday and the day of Fitr gather in one day, then let him combine them; let him pray two rak'ahs only where he prays the Fitr prayer, then that is it until Asr. Then he informed me at that, he said: A day of Fitr and a Friday gathered in one day in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr, so Ibn al-Zubayr said: Two Eids gathered in one day, so he combined them together making them one, and prayed on Friday two rak'ahs early, the Fitr prayer, then did not add to it until he prayed Asr. He said: As for the jurists they did not speak about that, and as for one without understanding he condemned him for it. He said: And I indeed condemned him for it, and prayed Zuhr on that day. He said: Until it reached us afterwards that when the two Eids gathered like that they were prayed as one. And that was mentioned from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn; he reported that they were combined when they gathered. They said: He found it in a book of Ali, he claimed."
"5564","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, regarding Ibn al-Zubayr's combining between them on the day he combined between them."
"5565","He said: We heard that, that Ibn Abbas said: Right, two Eids gathered in one day."
"5566","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: One of them suffices for the other."
"5567","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz, from Dhakwan, he said: Two Eids gathered in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Fitr and Jumu'ah, or Adha and Jumu'ah. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and said: You have attained remembrance and good, and we shall gather; whoever wishes to gather let him gather, and whoever wishes to sit let him sit."
"5568","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Some people of Madinah informed me, from more than one of them: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his time a Friday and a day of Fitr gathered, or a Friday and Adha, so he prayed the first Eid with the people, then preached, then permitted the Ansar to return to al-Awali and leave Jumu'ah, and the matter remained upon that afterwards."
"5569","Ibn Jurayj said: And it was narrated to me from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and from Abu Salih al-Zayyat: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his time a Friday and a day of Fitr gathered, so he said: This day is a day in which two Eids have gathered, so whoever loves let him return, and whoever loves to wait let him wait."
"5570","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, that they gathered while Ali was at Kufah, so he prayed then prayed Jumu'ah, and said when he prayed Fitr: Whoever was here, we have permitted him, as if to those around him meaning Jumu'ah."
"5571","From al-Thawri, from Abdullah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, he said: Two Eids gathered in one day, so he said: Whoever wishes to gather let him gather, and whoever wishes to sit let him sit. Sufyan said: Meaning he sits in his house."
"5572","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Ubayd the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: I attended Uthman when Fitr and Jumu'ah gathered, so Uthman preached to the people after the prayer, then said: These two Eids have gathered in one day, so whoever is from the people of al-Awali and loves to stay until he attends Jumu'ah let him do, and whoever loves to leave we have permitted him."
"5573","From Ma'mar, from a companion of his: That Ali, when they gathered in one day, used to pray Eid in the first of the day, and pray Jumu'ah in the last of the day."
"5574","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, he said: He heard Ibn Abbas saying: If you are able that none of you goes out on the day of Fitr until he eats, let him do. He said: So I never left eating before going out since I heard that from Ibn Abbas; I eat from the edge of al-Sarifah. We said to him: What is al-Sarifah? He said: Thin bread, one meal, or I drink milk, or nabidh, or water. I said: Upon what do you interpret this?"
"5575","He said: I heard him, he said: I think from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: They would not go out until forenoon extended, and they would say: We eat so that we do not hasten away from the prayer. He said: And sometimes I went out early and tasted nothing but water; Ibn Abbas is the speaker."
"5576","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: A person used to be commanded to eat on the day of Fitr before the imam goes out to the prayer place. Ma'mar said: So al-Zuhri used to eat on the day of Fitr before going out, and would not eat on the day of sacrifice until they sacrificed."
"5577","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: He used to eat on the day of Fitr before going out."
"5578","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, or from one who heard Ali, I doubt, from Ali: That he would not go out on the day of Fitr until he ate; he used to command that."
"5579","From Abu Hanifah, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to like to eat on the day of Fitr before going out to the prayer place."
"5580","From Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: I saw Amir al-Sha'bi on the day of Fitr while we were with him, and his neighbours gathered to him, so he went out with a loaf in his hand, and gave every person a piece and he ate it, then set out to the mosque, or he said: to the prayer place."
"5581","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar used to go out on the day of Fitr from the mosque. He said: And I do not know that he ate anything."
"5582","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The people used to eat on the day of Fitr before going out."
"5583","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, and al-Aswad, that Ibn Mas'ud said: Do not eat before going out on the day of Fitr if you wish."
"5584","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi': That Ibn Umar would not eat on the day of Fitr."
"5585","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: The siwak on Friday is sunnah."
"5586","From Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sabrah, from his father, he said: I mentioned to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz on the day Uthman ibn Affan descended from the minbar on Friday, and his statement: O people, I forgot the siwak, so he descended and cleaned his teeth, then returned to the minbar, so Umar said: Indeed it is from the Sunnah regarding the siwak on the day of Eid, like its manner on Friday."
"5587","Abu Bakr said, and Amr ibn Sulaym informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: The siwak on the day of Eid is sunnah."
"5588","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Cleaning the teeth on the day of Fitr? He said: It has not reached me that it was commanded on the day of Fitr specifically, but it reached us from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have commanded them with the siwak for every prayer."
"5589","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah, he said: He used to bathe on the day of Fitr before going out."
"5590","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that he used to command bathing on the day of Fitr, and say: It is not obligatory but it is good and recommended."
"5591","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Bathing on the day of Fitr is good because it is a day of Eid, and I would not leave bathing on the day of Fitr. I said: Is ghusl sought therein as ghusl for janabah is sought? He said: No."
"5592","From Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Ibn al-Musayyib, and Nadrah, they said: Ghusl on the day of the two Eids is sunnah. He said: And Ibn al-Musayyib said: Like the ghusl of janabah."
"5593","From a man of Aslam, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father: That Ali used to bathe on the day of Fitr and the day of Adha before going out. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the same, and he added: and perfume himself."
"5594","From Malik, from Nafi': That Ibn Umar used to bathe on the day of Fitr before going out. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I do it."
"5595","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: I never saw Ibn Umar bathe for Eid at all; he would spend the night of Fitr in the mosque, then go out from it when he prayed Fajr, and not come to his house."
"5596","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd al-Mukatib, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to pray Fajr with their clothes on them, then go out to the prayer place on the day of Fitr. Sufyan said: Whoever does that, I prefer that he bathe before dawn."
"5597","From a man of the people of Basra, from Abu Sinan, from al-Shaybani, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: I bathe on the day of Fitr, and the day of sacrifice, and the day of Arafah, and Friday, and from janabah and wet dream, and from the bathhouse, and when I am cupped."
"5598","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I like to give the Fitr charity with the measure of today, a measure we take with and live upon."
"5599","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: That he used to give the Fitr charity with the mudd with which his family lives."
"5600","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I were in a city other than my city and their measure were bigger than my measure, do I pay Fitr with it, or do I pay with the measure of my city? He said: Nothing is upon you but that, and increase of good is good. He said: How much reached you between the measure of today and the measure that was in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman? He said: I do not know except that that measure is smaller."
"5601","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, that the mudd of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a third of the mudd which Marwan ibn al-Hakam made. Ibn Jurayj said: Then Abu Bakr informed me, he said: With us it is four and a half ratls."
"5602","Ibn Jurayj said: And Hisham ibn Urwah informed me: That he used to pay his charity with the mudd with which he ate, and the mudd of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with which charities were taken in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a ratl and a half."
"5603","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Fitr charity is upon every free and slave, male and female, small and big, rich and poor: a sa' of dates, or half a sa' of wheat. Ma'mar said: And it reached me that al-Zuhri used to raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5604","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated the Fitr charity upon male and female, free and slave: a sa' of dates, or a sa' of barley. Ibn Umar said: So the people afterwards made it equal to two mudds of wheat. Ibn Umar said: And he liked to give dates."
"5605","From al-Thawri, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar. And from Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the Fitr charity upon every free, slave Muslim, small and big: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley. Ibn Abi Layla said in his hadith from Nafi': Ibn Umar said: So the people afterwards made it equal to two mudds of wheat. From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, like the hadith of Ubaydullah."
"5606","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Upon every man, slave or free, or free woman, or slave woman, and the people in that are equal, small and big, except traded slaves: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of barley or dates. Ata said: So pay on behalf of your slave, and if the slave pays on behalf of himself it suffices his master."
"5607","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that he heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying upon the minbar: The Fitr charity is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley; free and slave are equal."
"5608","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Fitr charity is upon every slave or free, small and big; whoever pays raisins it is accepted from him, and whoever pays dates it is accepted from him, and whoever pays barley it is accepted from him, and whoever pays sult it is accepted from him, a sa' a sa'."
"5609","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me: And it reached me from Ibn Abbas that he said: The Fitr charity is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."
"5610","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah informed me, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Alqamah, and al-Aswad, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."
"5611","From Ma'mar, and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: Upon free and slave two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates, and maize is double wheat."
"5612","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: Everything besides wheat is a sa', and wheat is half a sa'."
"5613","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah saying: The Fitr charity upon every small and big Muslim, slave or free, is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."
"5614","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-A'la, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, he said: Upon whomsoever your spending applies, half a sa' of wheat, or a sa' of dates."
"5615","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from Abu Qilabah, he said: A man informed me: That Abu Bakr al-Siddiq attached to him half a sa' of wheat between two men."
"5616","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa informed me, that Nafi' informed him, from Ibn Umar, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding the Fitr charity: a sa' of dates, or a sa' of barley. Abdullah said: So the people made two mudds of wheat its equal."
"5617","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Qilabah, he said: One who paid to Abu Bakr informed me: Half a sa' of wheat between two men."
"5618","From Ma'mar, he said: It reached me: That Abu Bakr paid the Fitr charity two mudds."
"5619","From Ma'mar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote: Upon every two a dirham, meaning the Fitr charity. Ma'mar said: This is according to what is given of measure."
"5620","From Dawud ibn Qays, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh narrated to me, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: We used to pay, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us, the Fitr charity, upon every small and big, free and owned: a sa' of dried curds, a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, and we kept paying it like that until Mu'awiyah came as pilgrim or performer of umrah, and spoke to the people upon the minbar, and among what he spoke to them was that he said: I see two mudds of Syrian wheat equal a sa' of dates, so the people took two mudds. Abu Sa'id said: As for me, I shall keep paying it as I used to pay it ever."
"5621","From al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh narrated to me, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: The Fitr charity in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, a sa' of dried curds, then when Mu'awiyah came, Syrian wheat came, so he held that two mudds equal a mudd."
"5622","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Iyad ibn Abdullah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We used to pay the Fitr charity in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, until Mu'awiyah's time, and wheat became abundant so it was paid."
"5623","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: A man used to be commanded to pay, before going out, a sa' of dates or half a sa' of wheat."
"5624","From Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Mijlaz: That Ibn Umar liked to give dates."
"5625","From Bakkar ibn Abdullah, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I asked Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and Sa'id ibn Jubayr, about the food of Fitr, and they said: A sa' of dates, or a sa' of barley, or a mudd of wheat."
"5626","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abdullah ibn Tha'labah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached to the people a day or two before Fitr, and said: Pay a sa' of wheat or grain between two, or a sa' of dates, or a sa' of barley upon every one, small or big."
"5627","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Fitr charity upon whoever fasted is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates."
"5628","Ma'mar said, and one who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: There is no charity except upon whoever fasted or prayed."
"5629","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Dhubab informed me, from Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We used to pay the Fitr charity in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from three kinds: barley, dried curds, and dates. Iyad said: I said to him: What about wheat? He said: It became abundant afterwards, so it was paid in the time of Mu'awiyah."
"5630","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: They liked to give the Fitr charity on behalf of small and big, even for the fetus in its mother's belly."
"5631","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A fetus not moving in its mother's belly, do I pay charity on it? He said: No, because you do not know whether it completes or not; whether it comes out dead or alive."
"5632","From Malik ibn Anas, from a man, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: I asked him about pregnancy, is charity paid for it? He said: Yes."
"5633","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: It was recommended for the people of the desert to go out on the day of Eid, so one of them leads them, and they pay the Fitr charity."
"5634","From Zam'ah, from Salih, he said: Muhammad ibn Ata ibn Yuhannis informed me, from his maternal uncle Abu al-Abbas al-Mudliji, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr sat upon the minbar a day or two before Fitr, and said: The Fitr charity upon every Muslim is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates; let a man pay on behalf of himself, and on behalf of his child, and on behalf of his slaves. Abu al-Abbas said: So I said: And upon the people of the desert? He said: Yes, were they not Muslims, and I do not think them but Muslims. From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Abu al-Abbas, from Abu al-Zubayr the same."
"5635","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Our valleys Marr, and Nakhlah, and Arafah, is the Fitr charity upon them? He said: Yes. I said: With us or with them? He said: Rather with us."
"5636","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, that Ibn al-Musayyib said regarding the people of the deserts: Qad aflaha man tazakka. Umar said, Qatadah said: Qad aflaha man tazakka, he said: With righteous deed."
"5637","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you see His statement: Qad aflaha man tazakka is for Fitr? He said: It is about charity altogether."
"5638","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Upon the people of the desert from charity? He said: No, I did not hear of it except upon the people of villages."
"5639","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: The people of the desert are themselves shepherds of their livestock and its workers, meaning the tent-dwellers."
"5640","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Fitr charity is sunnah; it is upon the people of the deserts."
"5641","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, he said: Qasamah existed in Jahiliyyah in blood, and in a child born upon one's bed claimed by another man, so they would swear upon him fifty oaths, like the qasamah of blood, then take him. Then when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj, al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said to him: So-and-so is my son, and we shall swear upon him. So the Prophet said: No, the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Then he sent a crier crying among the people of Makkah: Verily the Fitr charity is a due obligation upon every Muslim, male and female, free or slave, small or big, present or desert-dweller: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of other food. Verily the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is al-athlab, meaning the stone. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed the qasamah of blood as it was in Jahiliyyah."
"5642","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah narrated to me, from Abu Ammar, he said: We asked Sa'd ibn Qays ibn Ubadah about the Fitr charity, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us with it before zakat was revealed; then when zakat was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we do it."
"5643","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If one has traded slaves, let him not pay on them. And al-Thawri said it."
"5644","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: If they fasted with you Ramadan until they break fast, then feed on their behalf."
"5645","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Pay on behalf of your slave, for the slave's paying on behalf of himself suffices his master; and if he is a mukatab and pays on behalf of himself, it suffices his master; and if he does not pay on behalf of himself, let his master pay on his behalf, for he is a slave until freed. And if you were absent on the day of Fitr, then when you come pay charity on behalf of yourself; and if you have Christian slaves not traded, pay on their behalf; and pay on behalf of your travelling slave."
"5646","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar had two mukatabs and he would not pay the Fitr charity on their behalf. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi' the same."
"5647","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: A man does not pay the Fitr charity on behalf of his mukatab if he wishes."
"5648","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, regarding Christian slaves, he said: Not traded. He said: They are wealth, so let payment be made on their behalf. Abd al-Razzaq said: Yudarun means traded."
"5649","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said, al-Hasan also said: Do not pay except on behalf of whoever prayed and fasted."
"5650","From al-Thawri, from Abu Abd al-Karim, from Ibrahim, he said: A man feeds on behalf of his slave, even if he is Christian."
"5651","From Thawr ibn Yazid, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Ata, he said: A man feeds on behalf of his slave, even if he is Magian."
"5652","From a man of Aslam, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A man pays the Fitr charity on behalf of his mukatab, and on behalf of every slave of his, even if Jewish or Christian."
"5653","From a man of Aslam, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ja'far, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We used to pay the Fitr charity on behalf of every soul we maintain, even if Christian."
"5654","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If your slave has small free sons, their father does not pay charity on their behalf except with his master's permission. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, like the statement of Ata, he said: He does not pay on their behalf except with his master's permission."
"5655","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Abbas ibn Abdullah ibn Ma'bad informed me, he said: It reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let every person among you, small or big, free or owned, poor or rich, pay half a sa' of wheat, or a sa' of dates. As for our poor, there returns to him more than what was taken from him, and as for the rich, it is found."
"5656","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Zakat al-fitr was due upon every rich person and poor person."
"5657","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: A man said to Ata: He pays zakat al-fitr on behalf of himself and his dependents; may he take from it when it is distributed? He said: Yes, if he is needy."
"5658","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A poor needy man who is in debt, should he pay it? He said: Yes. Then a man said: May he take from it? He said: Yes."
"5659","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The destitute is given zakat al-fitr even if he has received it."
"5660","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: If the poor person receives zakah on the day of Fitr, he does not pay it on behalf of himself."
"5661","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Have you seen a poor person who cannot find means, should he beg in order to pay it? He said: No; it is only upon one who has means."
"5662","From al-Thawri, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, he said: If the poor person receives zakah on the day of Fitr, he does not pay it on behalf of himself. And Waki, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the same."
"5663","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked: Is there zakah upon a slave boy regarding a walled garden or livestock? He said: No, because the wealth in which he is has already had sadaqah paid upon it."
"5664","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umayyah ibn Abi Uthman informed me, from Umayyah ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid, that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan wrote to Ibn Alqamah regarding a slave who is among livestock or a walled garden: there is no zakat al-fitr upon him, because the garden and livestock in which he is have had sadaqah paid upon them."
"5665","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to pay zakah, or he said: pay it, on behalf of the workers of his land."
"5666","From Maamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to pay zakat al-fitr in Madinah on behalf of his slaves who worked in his land, on behalf of his wifes slaves, and on behalf of every person he maintained."
"5667","From Maamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to pay zakah on behalf of his slaves who were in his land and among his livestock."
"5668","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I know it only from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: It is upon the shepherds."
"5669","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, that he asked Ibn al-Musayyib, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman about a mans slaves among his livestock; they said: Let him give food on their behalf."
"5670","From a man of Aslam, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, from Makhul, that Muadh ibn Jabal and Ibn Masud said: There is no zakat al-fitr upon field workers and the lowly rabble. And Ibn Umar said: It is upon the shepherds, meaning: upon the workers of the slaves of the livestock."
"5671","From Maamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to pay zakat al-fitr on behalf of every slave of his, present or absent, or in a farm, until he would perhaps pay on behalf of sixty or seventy. Abd al-Razzaq said: And upon the Bedouins is milk, meaning: in zakah."
"5672","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: If you are able, then give your zakah before the prayer, or in front of the prayer, meaning the Fitr prayer."
"5673","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: When do you order your food to be given? He said: I go early at dawn and order it, and it is taken out after me before the prayer."
"5674","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He said: We heard it said: Order your food when you go out for the prayer, and let it be taken."
"5675","From Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar used to send the sadaqah of Ramadan when those who receive it sat to collect, and that was a day or two before Fitr."
"5676","From Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to send the sadaqah of Ramadan when those who receive it sat to collect, and that was a day or two before Fitr."
"5677","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to give out zakat al-fitr before going out to the prayer place, when those who receive it sat to collect, and that was a day or two before Fitr."
"5678","From Maamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: It was commanded that zakah be given before going out to the prayer place."
"5679","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no harm in paying zakat al-fitr a day or two before it, or a day or two after Fitr. He said: And he himself used to give it out before going early to prayer."
"5680","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is there any harm if I delay it until after Fitr? He said: No."
"5681","Reported from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar informed me, he said: I met Salim ibn Abd Allah and others of our scholars and elders, and they would not give it out except when going early to prayer. And I heard it from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar."
"5682","He said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded zakat al-fitr before the people go out to the prayer place."
"5683","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, that it reached him: That the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and commanded the people to give it out before they go out to the prayer place, as a sunnah."
"5684","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: The people used to give their zakah and eat before going out to the prayer place."
"5685","From Maamar, he said: Al-Hasan was asked about zakat al-fitr, and he ordered us to give it out. It was said: But they exact it from us. He said: Do not hand it over to them, and do not gratify them with it."
"5686","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, that Abu Ishaq informed them both, that Amr ibn Shurahbil used to gather zakat al-fitr in the mosque of his quarter, then send it up to the devotees. Al-Thawri said: And others than him used to give it to the Muslims."
"5687","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Ayyub used to send zakat al-fitr to his neighbours in dishes."
"5688","From Maamar, from Qatadah, that he permitted people who were not near the congregational mosque in Basrah to give their zakah, zakat al-fitr, to the needy among their relatives. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: Do you pay it in the congregational mosque? He said: If they do not store it, yes; but if I know they store it, I distribute it among my neighbours. We said to him: Maamar used to send it to the mosque, and at that time they did not store it."
"5689","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: It was liked for the people of the desert to go out on the day of Eid, and one of them lead them in prayer."
"5690","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If the people of the desert wish, they need not pray the Fitr prayer except in a congregational town."
"5691","From a man of Aslam, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent to the villages of Uraynah, Fadak, Yanbu and similar villages at three days distance from Madinah, that they establish Friday prayer and attend the two Eids."
"5692","From Hushaym, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Anas, from his grandfather Anas ibn Malik, that when he was in his house at al-Zawiyah, if he did not attend Eid in Basrah, he would gather his family, children and freedmen, then order his freedman Abd Allah ibn Abi Utbah, and he prayed two rakahs with them."
"5693","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ali ibn Abi Humayd informed me that Tawus would not leave a black slave girl of his nor any other but he ordered them to dye their hands and feet for the day of Fitr and the day of Adha, saying: It is a day of Eid."
"5694","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to dye with henna after the last Isha until dawn."
"5695","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Jafar ibn Muhammad, and said: It reached me that you narrated from your father that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to wear a cloak for each of the two Eids. He said: I did not say that, but I was informed from my father that he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wore a suit, or a cloak, in the Farewell Hajj on the day of Arafah."
"5696","Abu Said Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Bishr al-Arabi reported to us, he said: Abu Yaqub Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, he said. He said: Ibn Jurayj reported to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The prostrations of the Quran are ten: al-Araf, al-Nahl, al-Rad, Bani Israil, Maryam, al-Hajj, al-Furqan, Ta-Sin the middle one, Alif-Lam-Mim al-Tanzil, and Ha-Mim al-Sajdah. I said: Did Ibn Abbas not say there is a sajdah in Sad? He said: No."
"5697","He said: Ibn Jurayj reported to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid reported to us, that Said ibn Jubayr informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas and Ibn Umar counting how many sajdahs are in the Quran, and they said: al-Araf, al-Nahl, al-Rad, Bani Israil, Maryam, al-Hajj its first one, al-Furqan, Ta-Sin, Alif-Lam-Mim al-Tanzil, Sad, and Ha-Mim al-Sajdah, eleven."
"5698","From Maamar, from Abu Jamrah al-Dubai, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: In the Quran there are eleven sajdahs, and he counted them as Ibn Jurayj mentioned from Ikrimah from Said ibn Jubayr."
"5699","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me that Mujahid informed him that he asked Ibn Abbas: Is there prostration in Sad? He said: Yes, then he recited: And We gave him, until he reached: So follow their guidance, Surah al-Anam verses 84 to 90. He said: He is among them."
"5700","And Ibn Abbas said: I saw Umar recite Sad upon the pulpit, then come down and prostrate in it, then go back up upon the pulpit."
"5701","From Maamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali. And al-Thawri mentioned it from Asim also, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali: The binding ones are four: Alif-Lam-Mim al-Tanzil, Ha-Mim al-Sajdah, al-Najm, and Read in the name of your Lord the Most High Who created. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I prostrate in all the binding ones, meaning the binding ones: it has been made binding upon you to prostrate in them. Abu Bakr said: And I prostrate in them and in all prostrations when I am alone."
"5702","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, he said: I saw Uthman prostrate in Sad."
"5703","He said: Maamar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prostrate in Sad, and Sad is not among the binding ones."
"5704","From al-Thawri, from al-Suddi, from Abu Malik, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, recited upon the pulpit, then came down and prostrated."
"5705","From Israil, from a man, from Abu Mabad, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: I saw Ibn Abbas prostrate in Sad."
"5706","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, that he heard Ibn Abbas asked: Is there a sajdah in Sad? He said: Yes; Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance."
"5707","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Muzani, that a man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw as if a man was writing the Quran and a tree opposite him; when he passed the place of sajdah in Sad, it prostrated and said: O Allah, bring about for me gratitude thereby, magnify for me reward thereby, and remove thereby a burden. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then we are more entitled than the tree."
"5708","From Maamar, from Umar ibn Dharr, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said regarding the sajdah of Sad: Dawud prostrated it in repentance, and I prostrated it in gratitude."
"5709","From Maamar, from Ibn Tawus, that his father used to prostrate in Sad."
"5710","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: I heard Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah saying: I heard Ibn Umar saying: In Sad there is a sajdah."
"5711","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: It is only the repentance of a Prophet that was mentioned, and he would not prostrate in it, meaning Sad."
"5712","From Said al-Zubaydi, from Fitr, from Mujahid, that Ibn Abbas used to prostrate at the latter one of Ha-Mim: And they do not tire."
"5713","He said: Maamar reported to us, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, that Ibn Abbas said to a man who prostrated at the first one, If it is Him you worship: You hastened."
"5714","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to prostrate at the latter one, And they do not tire."
"5715","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Ibrahim, that he used to prostrate at it, And they do not tire."
"5716","From Maamar, from Qatadah, that al-Hasan used to prostrate at the first one, If it is Him you worship."
"5717","From Maamar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard him mention from some of them that he used to prostrate at the first one, If it is Him you worship."
"5718","From Malik and Maamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, that Umar prostrated in al-Najm, stood up and joined another surah to it."
"5719","From Maamar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prostrate in al-Najm, and the people prostrated with him. Al-Muttalib said: And I did not prostrate with them; he was at that time a polytheist. Al-Muttalib said: I will never leave prostrating in it ever. And by this we take."
"5720","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Husayn ibn Sabrah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he recited Yusuf in Fajr, then bowed, then recited in the second al-Najm, stood up and prostrated, then recited: When the earth is shaken with its shaking."
"5721","From al-Thawri, from Isam, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, that Ammar prostrated in When the sky splits open."
"5722","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I saw Umar and Abd Allah prostrating in When the sky splits open. Then he said: Or one of them. And by this we take."
"5723","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, that Abu Hurayrah used to prostrate in When the sky splits open."
"5724","From Maamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Abu Hurayrah used to prostrate in it."
"5725","And Abu Hurayrah said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prostrating in it."
"5726","Al-Thawri and Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ayyub, from Musa, from Ata ibn Mina, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in When the sky splits open and Read in the name of your Lord Who created."
"5727","From Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, from Amir al-Shabi, he said: I prostrate in When the sky splits open."
"5728","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Mulaykah reported to us, from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, from Rabiah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hudayr, that he was present when Umar ibn al-Khattab on Friday recited Surah al-Nahl upon the pulpit; when he came to the sajdah, he came down and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. The next Friday he recited it, and when he came to the sajdah, he said: O people, we only pass by the sajdah; whoever prostrates has done right and well, and whoever does not prostrate, there is no sin upon him. He said: And Umar did not prostrate."
"5729","Ibn Jurayj said: And Nafi added to me, from Ibn Umar, that he said: Prostration has not been made obligatory upon us except if we wish."
"5730","From Maamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Umar and Ibn Umar used to prostrate two sajdahs in al-Hajj. He said: And Ibn Umar said: If I prostrated one in it, the latter sajdah would be dearer to me. He said: And Ibn Umar said: This surah has been distinguished with two sajdahs."
"5731","From Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, he said: I saw Ibn Umar prostrating two sajdahs in al-Hajj."
"5732","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Ala, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: In Surah al-Hajj, the first is a binding one and the latter is instruction, and he would not prostrate in it."
"5733","He said: Maamar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, when he recited al-Najm, would prostrate in it while in prayer; if he did not prostrate, he would bow."
"5734","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Surah al-Hajj has been distinguished with two sajdahs."
"5735","From al-Thawri, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, he said: One who saw Umar at al-Jabiyah informed me that he prostrated twice in al-Hajj."
"5736","From Maamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to prostrate in When the sky splits open."
"5737","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: Nafi narrated to me that Ibn Umar, when he recited al-Najm, would prostrate, and when he recited In the name of your Lord Who created in prayer, he would say takbir, bow and prostrate, and when he recited them outside prayer, he would prostrate in both."
"5738","He said: Ibn Jurayj reported, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: When you prostrate in a sajdah, do not bow until you recite verses after it."
"5739","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata ibn Yasar, that he asked Zayd ibn Thabit about al-Najm, is there a sajdah in it? Zayd said: I recited it before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he did not prostrate."
"5740","From Maamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no sajdah in the Mufassal. From Maamar, from Abu Jamrah al-Dubai, from Ibn Abbas, the same."
"5741","From Maamar, from one who heard Anas and al-Hasan saying: There is no sajdah in the Mufassal. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, the same."
"5742","From one who heard Ikrimah narrating, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prostrated in the Mufassal when he was in Makkah; he says: Then he did not prostrate afterwards."
"5743","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is prostration obligatory? He said: No. It reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab, while he was reciting a surah containing a sajdah, those around him prostrated, and he said: Had you not prostrated, I would not have prostrated, and it is not in prayer."
"5744","From al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Uthman passed by a storyteller who recited a sajdah so that Uthman would prostrate with him, and Uthman said: Prostration is only upon one who listens attentively. Then he passed on and did not prostrate. Al-Zuhri said: And Ibn al-Musayyib used to sit in a corner of the mosque, and the storyteller would recite the sajdah, and he would not prostrate with him, saying: I did not sit for it."
"5745","From Maamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn Hanzalah, he said: I recited the sajdah before Ibn Masud, and I looked at him, and he said: What are you looking at? You recited it; if you prostrate, we will prostrate."
"5746","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Prostration is only upon one who sat for it; if you pass by and they prostrate, there is no prostration upon you."
"5747","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Salman passed by a people sitting, and they recited the sajdah and prostrated. It was said to him, and he said: We did not come early for it."
"5748","From Maamar, or another, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah, that Imran ibn al-Husayn passed by a storyteller, and the storyteller recited a sajdah, and Imran passed on and did not prostrate with him, and said: Prostration is only upon one who sat for it."
"5749","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to recite the Quran to us; when he passed a sajdah, he would say takbir and prostrate, and we would prostrate with him."
"5750","From Maamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Umar recited upon the pulpit a surah containing a sajdah, then came down and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him. In the next Friday he recited that surah, and when he reached near the sajdah, the people prepared for prostration, and he said: It is not binding upon us except if we wish. So he recited it and did not prostrate."
"5751","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is prostration obligatory in prayer? He said: No. Then he said: If it were obligatory upon you in prayer, it would be obligatory upon you in recitation. I said: Which is dearer to you? He said: Prostration."
"5752","From Maamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: A man recited a surah containing a sajdah before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; when he finished, he said: O Messenger of Allah, is there no sajdah in this surah? He said: Yes, but you were the imam; had you prostrated, we would have prostrated. And Ibn Jurayj said it from Ata."
"5753","From Muhammad ibn Ammar, and more than one, from Asim, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Aishah was asked about the prostrations of the Quran, and she said: A due of Allah which you render, or a voluntary act you do voluntarily; there is no Muslim who prostrates a prostration to Allah but Allah raises him a degree thereby, or removes thereby a sin from him, or combines both for him. From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Zayd, the same."
"5754","He said: Al-Awzai reported, from al-Walid ibn Hisham, from Khalid ibn Madan, he said: I said to Thawban: Narrate to me a hadith by which Allah may benefit me. I said that to him three times, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: There is no servant who prostrates a prostration to Allah but Allah raises him a degree thereby and removes thereby a sin from him."
"5755","He said: Maamar reported, from Abu Ishaq, I heard him saying: Ibn Masud said: If the sajdah is at the end of the surah, then bow if you wish, or prostrate, for the sajdah goes with the bow. I said: Who narrated this to you, O Abu Ishaq? He said: Our companions Alqamah, al-Aswad, and al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym."
"5756","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abd Allah, he said: If the sajdah is the seal of the surah, if you wish you bow, and if you wish you prostrate."
"5757","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who heard a woman recite a sajdah, he said: Let him not take her as imam, but let him recite it then prostrate."
"5758","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: al-Araf, Bani Israil, Read in the name of your Lord, al-Najm, and When the sky splits open: if he wishes he bows, and if he wishes he prostrates."
"5759","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: I know it only from Ibn Masud, he said: When you pass al-Najm, When the sky splits open, Read in the name of your Lord Who created, Bani Israil, and the end of al-Araf, if you wish you prostrate, then join to it something of the Quran, and if you wish you bow."
"5760","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When you reach the sajdah, if you wish you make it a bow. Ibn Jurayj said, and Ibn Tawus said it."
"5761","From Maamar, from Ibn Tawus, that his father would sometimes bow in Alif-Lam-Mim al-Tanzil when he reached the sajdah, and he would not leave reciting it every night."
"5762","From Maamar, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which mosque was placed on earth first? He said: The Sacred Mosque. He said: I said: Then which? He said: The Furthest Mosque. He said: I said: How long between them? He said: Forty years. Then he said: Wherever prayer reaches you, pray, for it is a mosque. Al-Taymi would sometimes recite Surah al-Sajdah while passing, and prostrate as he was upon the road."
"5763","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, regarding a man who recites the sajdah in prayer and prostrates, then adds another to it, he said: When he finishes, he prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"5764","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ata and Jabir, they said: When you recite the sajdah around the House, face the House and gesture with a gesture."
"5765","From Israil, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he would recite the sajdah while walking and gesture with a gesture."
"5766","From Israil, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, he said: When the imam recites the sajdah and does not prostrate, those behind him gesture."
"5767","He said: Maamar reported, from Qatadah, from Ibn Sirin and Abu Qilabah, that when they recited the sajdah, they would say takbir when they prostrated and say taslim when they finished."
"5768","From al-Thawri, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abu al-Ahwas, that he would say taslim in the sajdah."
"5769","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: He would recite to us while we were facing Bani Sulaym, to other than the qiblah, and he would pass the sajdah and gesture with a gesture, then say taslim."
"5770","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim, from Maamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: There is no taslim in prostration."
"5771","From Maamar, from Yahya ibn Shurahbil, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim, he said: I was with Ibn Umar, and a storyteller recited a sajdah after Fajr, and Ibn Umar shouted at him, and the storyteller prostrated, but Ibn Umar did not prostrate. When the sun rose, Ibn Umar made it up, saying: He prostrated it. And al-Thawri said: The sajdah is made up when you hear it and did not prostrate it."
"5772","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If you hear the sajdah while you are without wudu, then do tayammum then prostrate. From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: I heard Hammad narrating from Ibrahim, he said: He performs wudu and prostrates."
"5773","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Ibn Umar used to shout at them when he saw them, meaning the storytellers prostrating after Fajr. Maamar said, and Ayyub informed me of it from Nafi."
"5774","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If you hear the sajdah while you are praying, then prostrate; if you were bowing or prostrating, that suffices you for the sajdah."
"5775","From Maamar, from Jabir, he said: If you hear the sajdah while you are in prayer, then prostrate, unless you are prostrating."
"5776","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: Prayer indeed has enough occupation."
"5777","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Do not introduce into your prayer what is not part of it. Sufyan said: And we say: Make it up afterwards."
"5778","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is it disliked for a man to place a surah before a surah? He said: No."
"5779","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me, he said: I was with Aishah when an Iraqi came to her and said: Which shroud is best? She said: Woe to you, what harm to you? He said: O Mother of the Believers, show me your mushaf so that I may arrange the Quran upon it, for we recite it unarranged. She said: What harm to you whichever you recite first? Only a surah of the Mufassal was revealed containing mention of Paradise and Hell; when the people turned to Islam, the lawful and unlawful were revealed. Had the first thing revealed been: Do not drink wine, they would have said: We will never leave wine. Had it been revealed: Do not approach women, they would have said: We will never leave it. At Makkah was revealed, while I was a young girl playing, to Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: The Hour is more disastrous and more bitter. And Surah al-Baqarah was not revealed except while I was with him. He said: So she brought out the mushaf for him and dictated to him the verses of the surahs."
"5780","From Maamar, from Ayyub, or another, he said: Ibn Sirin used to recite the Quran in portions, then add to them another surah of the Quran, until he would sometimes add up to a seventh of the Quran, and he used to recite the Quran in seven days. Maamar said: And Qatadah used to recite it in seven."
"5781","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Ubayd Allah ibn Utbah, he said: Ibn Masud used to recite the Quran until he would hardly rest by day except a little."
"5782","From Maamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever recites the Quran in less than three days is babbling. From Maamar and al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Masud, the same."
"5783","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abd Allah said: Do not recite the Quran in less than three days; recite it in seven, and a man should guard his portion day and night."
"5784","From Maamar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, he said: I heard Ubayy ibn Kab: We recite it, or I recite it, in eight days."
"5785","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah: That Muadh ibn Jabal disliked reciting the Quran in less than three days."
"5786","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from a man of the Ansar, from his father, he said: I asked Zayd ibn Thabit about a man who recites the Quran in seven days. He said: Good, but to recite it in fifteen or twenty is dearer to me; I pause in it and reflect."
"5787","From Maamar, from a man, from Ibn Sirin, that Uthman used to recite the Quran in one rakah, keeping alive the night thereby. And Hisham mentioned it from Ibn Sirin the same."
"5788","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Said ibn Jubayr, he informed him that he recited the Quran in the Kabah in one rakah, and recited in the other rakah: Say: He is Allah, One. And al-Thawri said: There is no harm in reciting it in a night, if you understand its letters."
"5789","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he used to complete the Quran in two nights, and sleep between Maghrib and Isha in Ramadan."
"5790","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Imran, from Ibrahim, that he used to recite the Quran in Ramadan every three days; when the last ten entered, he would recite it in two nights, and bathe every night."
"5791","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrating, from Yahya ibn Hakim ibn Safwan, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: I had gathered the Quran and recited it in a night. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I fear time will be long upon you and you will become bored; recite it in a month. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and youth. He said: Recite it in twenty. He said: O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and youth. He said: Recite it in ten. He said: O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength and youth. He said: Recite it in seven. I said: O Messenger of Allah, let me enjoy my strength. But he refused."
"5792","Maamar reported, from Simak ibn al-Fadl, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, that he asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: In how many is the Quran recited? He said: In forty. He said: I can do more than that. He said: In a month. He said: I can do more than that. He said: In fifteen. Then he said: In ten. Then he said: In seven. He did not go below seven."
"5793","From Maamar, from Qatadah, that Abd Allah ibn Amr asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: In how many is the Quran recited? He said: In a month. He said: I can do more than that. He mentioned the like of the hadith of Simak until he ended at three. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever recites it in less than three has not understood it. Maamar said: And it reached me that whoever recites the Quran in a month has neither hastened nor delayed, and whoever recites it in twenty is like a well-trained racehorse."
"5794","From a man, from Shubah, from Said ibn Abi Burdah. He said: And Maamar mentioned part of it, from Said ibn Abi Burdah, he said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Musa, he said: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Muadh ibn Jabal and Abu Musa al-Ashari to Yemen, he said to them: Make easy and do not make difficult, do not separate, and obey one another."
"5795","Abu Musa said: A drink is made in our land from honey, called bita, and from barley, called mizr. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"5796","Muadh said to Abu Musa: How do you recite the Quran? He said: I recite it in my prayer, upon my mount, reclining and sitting, sipping it gradually. Muadh said: But I sleep, then stand and recite it, meaning his portion, and I seek reward for my sleep as I seek reward for my standing. It is as if Muadh ibn Jabal excelled over him."
"5797","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by an ugly short man called Zunaym, and fell prostrate, then raised his head and said: I ask Allah for well-being."
"5798","From Maamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, he said: When Allah turned to him and his repentance came down, he fell prostrate."
"5799","He said: Al-Thawri reported, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abu Musa al-Hamadhani, he said: I was with Ali on the day of al-Nahrawan, and he said: Seek Dhu al-Thudayyah. They sought him and could not find him. The forehead of Ali began to sweat, and he said: By Allah, I did not lie nor was I lied to; seek him. He said: We found him in a water channel or stream under the slain; Ali was brought to him, and he fell prostrate."
"5800","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Awn, he said: Abu Bakr prostrated when the conquest of Yamamah came to him. He said: Ibn Jurayj reported, from Abu Muhammad ibn Ali: That the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out and saw an ugly short man, and al-naghashi is the short one, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Thawri from Jabir."
"5801","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: Do not use the Qur'an as a pillow, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it slips away more severely than tied camels toward their resting place; and by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, there is no verse but it has an outer and an inner meaning, and there is no letter in it but it has a limit, and for every limit there is an approaching point. He said: So I narrated it to Ma'mar, and he said: Erase it, do not narrate it to anyone."
"5802","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Keep reciting the Qur'an regularly, for it slips away from a man's chest more severely than livestock from their hobbles. How evil it is for one of them to say: I have forgotten such-and-such verse; rather, he was made to forget."
"5803","From Ma'mar, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu al-Duha or Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Keep reciting the Qur'an regularly, for it is wild; it slips away more severely than camels from their hobbles. Let none of you say: I have forgotten such-and-such verse; rather, he was made to forget."
"5804","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah narrated to me that Shaqiq ibn Salamah said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: How evil it is for a man or a woman to say: I have forgotten such-and-such surah; rather, he was made to forget."
"5805","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Talq ibn Habib, he said: Whoever learns the Qur'an then forgets it without excuse, a rank is lowered from him for every verse, and he will come as one defeated in argument."
"5806","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of the Qur'an, when its keeper is faithful to it, reciting it by night and day, is like a man who has camels: if he hobbles them he preserves them, and if he loosens their hobbles they go away; likewise is the keeper of the Qur'an. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it."
"5807","From Ma'mar, from Aban, he mentioned it, from some of them, he said: There is no sin with which a servant will meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection, after what Allah has forbidden, greater than forgetting a surah he had memorized."
"5808","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no envy except toward two: a man to whom Allah has given the Qur'an and he stands with it through the hours of the night and day, and a man to whom Allah has given wealth and he spends from it, meaning charity and what resembles it, through the hours of the night and day."
"5809","From Ma'mar, from Hisham, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on so-and-so; how often he reminded me of a verse or verses that I had been made to forget."
"5810","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited one night Ha-Mim 'Ayn-Sin-Qaf and repeated it many times, Ha-Mim 'Ayn-Sin-Qaf, while he was in the house of Maymunah, and he said: O Maymunah, do you have Ha-Mim 'Ayn-Sin-Qaf with you? She said: Yes. He said: Then recite it to me, for I have been made to forget what is between its beginning and its end."
"5811","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: From a man, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rewards of my ummah were shown to me, even a speck or a dung pellet that a person removes from the mosque; and the sins of my ummah were shown to me, and I did not see a sin greater than a verse or surah given to a man and then he forgot it."
"5812","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: And it reached me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he said: For meteors to clash in my chest is dearer to me than dropping anything from the Qur'an."
"5813","He said: Al-Thawri informed us, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: Keep looking at the mushaf constantly; and if you differ over ya' and ta', then make them... remind me of the Qur'an."
"5814","From al-Thawri, from his father, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi', from Shaddad ibn Ma'qil. Al-Thawri said, and 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi' narrated to me, from Shaddad, that Ibn Mas'ud said: This Qur'an will surely be taken away from among you. He said: I said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, how will it be taken away when we have fixed it in our chests and fixed it in our mushafs? He said: It will be taken by night in one night, so nothing of it will remain in the heart of any servant nor in any mushaf, and people will wake up poor like cattle. Then 'Abdullah recited: And if We willed, We would surely take away what We revealed to you, then you would find no guardian for it against Us."
"5815","From Isra'il, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', from Shaddad ibn Ma'qil, he said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud say: The first thing you will lose of your religion is trustworthiness, and the last thing that will remain of your religion is prayer; and people who have no religion will surely pray, and the Qur'an will surely be taken away from among you. They said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, do we not recite the Qur'an, and have we not fixed it in our mushafs? He said: It will be taken by night, and it will be removed from the insides of men, so nothing of it will remain."
"5816","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning and said: There was a surah with me, and I went to recite it but I cannot recall it. Another said: And I too had one with me but I cannot recall it. He said: I do not know whether they were two men or three; they entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: It was raised up along with Qur'an that was raised up."
"5817","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far informed me that a delegation came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Makkah and asked him to leave them free for their need, and he said: My portion of the Qur'an escaped me tonight."
"5818","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from one who heard al-Hasan say: This Qur'an has been recited by children and slaves who have no knowledge of its interpretation, and they did not approach the matter from its beginning. And he said: A Book which We sent down to you, blessed, that they may ponder its verses; and pondering its verses is only following it with its knowledge. By Allah, it is not memorizing its letters while wasting its bounds, such that one of them says: By Allah, I have recited the whole Qur'an and have not dropped a single letter, while he has dropped all of it; you see in him no Qur'anic character or deed. And such that one of them says: By Allah, I recite a surah in a single breath. By Allah, these are not reciters, nor scholars, nor wise men, nor God-fearing. When were reciters ever saying such things? May Allah not increase such people among the Muslims."
"5819","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: 'Abdullah said: It is not a mistake to recite part of the Qur'an into another part, nor to end a verse of Ghafur Rahim with 'Alim Hakim or with 'Aziz Hakim; but the mistake is to recite what is not in it, or to end a verse of mercy with a verse of punishment."
"5820","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Darda', that he taught a man: The tree of Zaqqum, the food of the sinful. The man said: The food of the orphan. Abu al-Darda' said: The wicked one — meaning correct it as al-athim."
"5821","From al-Thawri, from Bayan, from Hakim ibn Jabir, from Abu al-Darda', he said: The most reciting of people of this Qur'an is the hypocrite; he does not leave out an alif nor a waw; he wraps it with his tongue as a cow wraps fodder with its tongue."
"5822","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: When one of you asks his companion how such-and-such verse is read, let him ask him about what is before it."
"5823","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Isa ibn Fa'id, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever learns the Qur'an then forgets it will meet Allah leprous."
"5824","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b said: How much do you recite of Surah al-Ahzab? He said: I said: Seventy-odd verses. He said: We used to recite it with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the length of Surah al-Baqarah, or it was longer; and we used to recite in it the verse of stoning: The old man and the old woman, stone them both outright as a punishment from Allah, and Allah is Mighty, Wise."
"5825","He said: Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Umamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Learn the Qur'an, for it is an intercessor for its companions on the Day of Resurrection; and learn al-Baqarah and Al 'Imran; learn the two radiant ones, for they will come on the Day of Resurrection as though they were two clouds, or two canopies, or as though they were two flocks of birds in ranks, arguing on behalf of their companion. And learn al-Baqarah, for learning it is blessing, and leaving it is regret, and the idle sorcerers cannot bear it — meaning by al-batalah: the sorcerers."
"5826","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: If it were said to one of you, if you went early to the village you would have four milking she-camels, he would say: It is time for me to go early. So if one of you went early and learned a verse from the Book of Allah, it would be better for him than four, and four, and four, until he counted a great many things."
"5827","Abu Ishaq said: And Abu 'Ubaydah informed me that when Ibn Mas'ud woke in the morning, he would go out and people would come to him at his house, and he would say: Stay in your places. Then he would pass by those to whom he taught the Qur'an and say: O so-and-so, which surah are you on? They would tell him, and he would say: Which verse? Then he would open for him the verse that follows it, then say: Learn it, for it is better for you than what is between heaven and earth. He said: So the man would think there is no verse in the Qur'an better than it. Then he would pass by the next one and say the like of that to him, until he said that to all of them."
"5828","He informed us, from Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Whoever recites the Qur'an, for him is ten good deeds for every verse. I do not say Alif-Lam-Mim is ten; but alif, lam, and mim are thirty good deeds."
"5829","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Husayn say: 'Abdullah ibn Marwan said: Allah chose speech, then chose the Qur'an; then He chose from it Surah al-Baqarah, and chose from Surah al-Baqarah Ayat al-Kursi; and He chose lands and chose the sanctuary, and chose the sanctuary and chose the mosque, and chose the mosque and chose the site of the House."
"5830","From al-Thawri, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are those who learn the Qur'an and teach it."
"5831","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Darda', that a man said to him: Your brothers among the people of Kufah send greetings to you. He said: And you, convey greetings to them, and say to them: Let them give the Qur'an their bridles, for it will carry them to moderation and ease, and will keep them from injustice and hardship — meaning by their bridles: make the Qur'an like the nose-rein on one of you, so follow it and act by it."
"5832","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hud and its sisters have made me grey: Surah al-Waqi'ah, Surah al-Qiyamah, al-Mursalat, When the sun is folded up, When the sky is split asunder, and When the sky is cleft. He said: And I think he mentioned Surah Hud."
"5833","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: This Qur'an is Allah's banquet, so whoever can learn something from it, let him do so; for the emptiest houses of good are the house in which there is nothing of the Book of Allah; and the house in which there is nothing of the Book of Allah is ruined like the ruin of a house with no dweller; and Satan leaves the house in which he hears Surah al-Baqarah recited."
"5834","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The house in which the Qur'an is recited increases in good, is made spacious for its people, the angels attend it, and the devils abandon it; and the house in which it is not recited is straitened upon its people, its good decreases, the angels abandon it, and the devils attend it. And the house in which the Qur'an is recited and studied deeply shines for the people of heaven as a star shines upon the earth."
"5835","He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give glad tidings to those who walk in darkness to the mosques of a light from Allah on the Day of Resurrection. Ma'mar said: And I heard a man from the people of Madinah say: The people of heaven surely see the house in which the Qur'an is recited and prayer performed, as the people of this world see a star in the sky."
"5836","From 'Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Three will recite the Qur'an: a man reciting it seeking Allah's pleasure and hoping for reward from Allah, so his reward is upon Allah; a man reciting it for show and repute to eat by it in this world, so it is against him and not for him; and a man reciting it and his recitation does not pass — or he said: his gullet — his collarbone."
"5837","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Salil, from 'Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which verse in the Book of Allah is greatest? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best, repeating it many times. Then Ubayy said: Ayat al-Kursi. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May knowledge delight you, Abu al-Mundhir; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it surely has a tongue and two lips sanctifying the King at the leg of the Throne."
"5838","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and others, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl al-'Absi, they both said: We sat in the mosque and people gathered to them, and one of them said to his companion: They have only come to us so that we narrate to them; either you narrate to them and I confirm you, or I narrate to them and you confirm me. One of them said: I heard 'Abdullah say: The greatest verse in the Qur'an is Ayat al-Kursi."
"5839","The other said: You have spoken the truth. The other said: I heard 'Abdullah say: The most comprehensive verse in the Qur'an is: Indeed Allah commands justice and excellence. He said: You have spoken the truth."
"5840","And I heard him say: The most strongly delegating verse in the Qur'an is: And whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out. He said: He said: You have spoken the truth."
"5841","He said: And I heard him say: The greatest verse in the Qur'an in relief is: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves. He said: You have spoken the truth."
"5842","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, he said: The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Qur'an."
"5843","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Qur'an."
"5844","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu Mulaykah informed me that he heard 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr say: Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Qur'an."
"5845","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: 'Ata' informed me that it reached him that: Say: He is Allah, the One, equals a third of the Qur'an."
"5846","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: Whoever recites: Say: O disbelievers, in a night has done much and well."
"5847","From Ja'far, from Hisham ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani say: When the earth is shaken is half the Qur'an, and Say: O disbelievers is a quarter of the Qur'an."
"5848","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard a man narrating: Everything has a heart, and the heart of the Qur'an is Ya-Sin; whoever recites it, it equals the Qur'an — or he said: equals reciting the whole Qur'an. And whoever recites: Say: O disbelievers, it equals a quarter of the Qur'an; and When shaken is half the Qur'an."
"5849","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq and others, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: 'Abdullah said: The Qur'an is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, and a prosecutor whose claim is believed; whoever puts it before him, it leads him to Paradise, and whoever puts it behind him, it drives him to the Fire."
"5850","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Qur'an is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, and a truthful prosecutor."
"5851","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: Whoever listens to a verse from the Book of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"5852","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Anas or from al-Hasan, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever listens to a verse from the Book of Allah, it will be a multiplied good deed for him; and whoever learns a verse from the Book of Allah, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"5853","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: It has reached us that the Qur'an will come on the Day of Resurrection in the form of a pale changed man, and will say to its companion: Do you know me? He will say: Who are you? It will say: I am your close friend, I am your bedfellow, I am your affectionate one; I am the one who kept your night sleepless and wearied your day, and moved with you wherever you moved. Every merchant has gained from his trade, and I today am for you beyond every merchant. Then he will be given dominion in his right hand and eternity in his left, the crown of dignity will be placed on his head, and it will be said to him: Go into lasting bliss. His parents will be clothed with two garments which the world could not match, and they will say: How is this, when we did not do it? It will be said: By your son's taking of the Qur'an. Then it will be said: Recite and ascend; whoever used to recite with measured recitation, then by that reckoning, and whoever used to recite hastily, then by that reckoning."
"5854","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from his companions, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: What an excellent treasure of the poor man is Surah Al 'Imran; he rises at the end of the night and stands with it in prayer."
"5855","He said: And 'Abdullah said: Whoever recites Al 'Imran is rich."
"5856","Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one skilled in the Qur'an is with the noble righteous scribes, and the one who recites it while it is hard upon him has two rewards."
"5857","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: This Qur'an is Allah's banquet, so learn from His banquet what you can; this Qur'an is the rope of Allah which He commanded, the clear light and the beneficial healing, a protection for whoever holds fast to it and salvation for whoever clings to it. It does not bend so as to need straightening, nor deviate so as to need correcting; its wonders do not end, and it does not wear out from repetition. Recite it, for Allah rewards you ten good deeds for every letter. I do not say to you: Alif-Lam-Mim; but alif is a letter, lam is a letter, and mim is a letter."
"5858","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, he said: Ibn Abi Labid narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a party and put the youngest of them in command over them, and they mentioned that, and he said: He is the most Qur'an-knowing among you. The likeness of the keeper of the Qur'an is only like a pouch containing musk: if you open it, or it is opened, its fragrance spreads; and if it is tied shut, it is tied over scent."
"5859","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Qur'an is Surah al-Baqarah; in it is a verse, the mistress of the Qur'an, Ayat al-Kursi; it is not recited in a house in which there is a devil but he leaves."
"5860","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, they suffice him. From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it; and he added: 'Abd al-Rahman said, and 'Alqamah narrated it to me, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: So I met Abu Mas'ud during tawaf and asked him about it, and he narrated it to me while circumambulating."
"5861","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever recites ten verses from the beginning of al-Kahf is protected from the trial of the Dajjal; and whoever recites its end — or he said: recites it to its end — it will be a light for him from his head to his feet."
"5862","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim al-Wasiti, from Abu Mijlaz, from Qays ibn 'Abbad, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: Whoever performs ablution then finishes his ablution, then says: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I bear witness there is no god but You; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You — it is sealed with a seal and placed beneath the Throne, and will not be broken until the Day of Resurrection. And whoever recites Surah al-Kahf as it was revealed then meets the Dajjal, he will have no power over him nor any way against him; and whoever recites the end of Surah al-Kahf, his light will shine from where he recited it, between him and Makkah."
"5863","Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud: A man died and the angels of punishment came to him and sat at his head, and it said: You have no way against him; he used to recite Surah al-Mulk to me. So they sat at his feet, and it said: You have no way; he used to stand over us reciting Surah al-Mulk. So they sat at his belly, and it said: You have no way against him; he had stored Surah al-Mulk in me. So it was named the Preventer."
"5864","From al-Thawri, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: A man is approached in his grave, and his feet are approached, and they both say: You have no way over what is on our side; he used to recite Surah al-Mulk over us. Then his belly is approached, and it says: You have no way over me; he had stored Surah al-Mulk in me. Then his head is approached, and it says: You have no way over what is on my side; he used to recite Surah al-Mulk with me. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And it is the Preventer; it prevents from the punishment of the grave. And in the Torah it is Surah al-Mulk; and whoever recites it in a night has done much and well."
"5865","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu al-Darda', he said: Whoever recites a hundred verses in a night, the Qur'an will not dispute against him."
"5866","From Ma'mar, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: When we used to learn ten verses of the Qur'an, we would not learn the ten after them until we learned their lawful and unlawful, their command and their prohibition."
"5867","From Ma'mar, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from 'Ata' al-Khurasani, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: Whoever recites a hundred verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless; whoever recites two hundred verses, the devotion of that night is recorded for him; and whoever recites five hundred to a thousand, he wakes with a qintar of reward. He said: So Ibn 'Umar was asked: How much is a qintar? He said: Seventy thousand. 'Amr said: And I heard al-Zuhri say: One who asked Ka'b about this saying of Ibn 'Umar informed me, and Ka'b said: But I say: Whoever prays 'Isha' at its time is not recorded among the heedless."
"5868","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Ata' informed me that two men in the past, one of them used to keep to: Blessed is He in Whose Hand is dominion, and it pleaded for him until he was saved; and as for the companion of the shorter prostration surah, it split in his grave into two parts: a part at his head and a part at his feet, until he was saved. So it was named the Dividing one."
"5869","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'Asim ibn 'Umar, that 'Umar would not command his sons to learn the Qur'an — if one of you is learning, let him learn from the Mufassal, for it is easier."
"5870","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The Al Ha-Mim surahs are the brocade of the Qur'an."
"5871","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of the Qur'an, when its keeper is faithful to it, supplicating and reciting through the hours of the night and the ends of the day, is like a man who has camels: if he hobbles them he preserves them, and if he loosens their hobbles they go away; likewise is the keeper of the Qur'an."
"5872","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: Ibn 'Abbas said: Whoever recites the Qur'an and follows what is in it, Allah guides him from misguidance in this world and shields him from reckoning on the Day of Resurrection; and that is because Allah says: Whoever follows My guidance shall neither go astray nor suffer."
"5873","From Ibn 'Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to people reciting the Qur'an and said: Recite, for every Book belongs to Allah — before there come people who set it up like the setting up of an arrow shaft, seeking it hastily and not deferring it."
"5874","From Ma'mar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his companions to recite Alif-Lam-Mim, the revelation, and Blessed is He in Whose Hand is dominion; for every verse of them equals seventy verses of others. Whoever recites them after the last 'Isha', they will be for him like them on Laylat al-Qadr."
"5875","From Ma'mar, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab addressed the people and said: A time came upon me when we used to think no one learned the Book of Allah except seeking Allah by it, until when later times came here, I thought people learn the Qur'an seeking people and what they have by it. So seek Allah in your deeds and your recitation; we used to know you while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us and revelation was descending and informing us of your news. As for today, I know you only by your speech: whoever displays good to us, we think good of him and love him for it; and whoever displays evil to us, we think evil of him and hate him for it. Your secrets are between you and Allah."
"5876","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to breathe in al-Hamd three times."
"5877","From Yunus ibn Sulaym al-San'ani, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd al-Qari, he said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab say: When revelation descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a sound like the buzzing of bees was heard at his face. It descended upon him, and we waited a while; then he faced the qiblah, raised his hands, and said: O Allah, increase us and do not decrease us; honor us and do not disgrace us; give us and do not deprive us; favor us and do not favor others over us; and be pleased with us. Then he said: Ten verses were revealed to me; whoever establishes them will enter Paradise. Then he recited to us: Successful indeed are the believers, until he completed the ten."
"5878","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from a man of Juhaynah, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, he said: While I was traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), verses were revealed to him the like of which I had never heard nor seen: the two Mu'awwidhatayn."
"5879","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about the Mu'awwidhatayn, and he said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: It was said to me, so I said. Ubayy said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me — so we say as he said."
"5880","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': Was it commanded to prompt the sick person with the talqin when death attended him? He said: I indeed love that."
"5881","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mansur ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his mother Safiyyah bint Shaybah, that she heard 'A'ishah say: Do not mention your dead except with good, and prompt them the testimony that there is no god but Allah."
"5882","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he said: Attend your dying ones, make them hold to: There is no god but Allah, close their eyes, and recite the Qur'an beside them."
"5883","From Ma'mar, from 'Umar ibn Qatadah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq had a son in whom there was something Abu Bakr was not pleased with, and he used to belittle him for that. He fell ill, and his father entered upon him, and the boy said to him: Send word to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a message: Convey to him from me that I bear witness there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. So Abu Bakr went until he entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Convey to your son that Paradise is his. He said: So Abu Bakr went out and met 'Umar and informed him, and 'Umar said to him: Return with us to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so we may confirm it from him. They returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said the like of this to him. 'Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, this is for the dead; then how about the living? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The like of it, and the like of it, and the like of it, until he counted some thirty times. He said: And al-Qasim gestured with his hand: thirty-four."
"5884","From al-Thawri, from Husayn and Mansur, or one of them, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Whoever says at his death: There is no god but Allah, it will save him one day of all time, whatever befell him before that."
"5885","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, he said: Prompt me with: There is no god but Allah, at my death; hasten me to my pit; and do not announce my death, for I fear it may be like the announcement of Jahiliyyah. When men go out with my bier, close the door, for I have no need of women."
"5886","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, raising it, he said: Prompt your dying ones with: There is no god but Allah."
"5887","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from one who narrated to him, that Ibn Mas'ud said: Prompt your dying ones with: There is no god but Allah, for it demolishes sins. It was said to him: How about the living? He said: It demolishes more and more."
"5888","From a companion of theirs, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard al-Agharr Abu Muslim narrating from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Five by which Allah confirms the servant when he says them: When he says: There is no god but Allah and Allah is Greatest, Allah says: My servant has spoken truth. When he says: There is no god but Allah and praise be to Allah, Allah says: My servant has spoken truth. When he says: There is no god but Allah, He has no partner, Allah says: My servant has spoken truth. When he says: There is no god but Allah alone, He says: My servant has spoken truth. And when he says: There is no god but Allah, to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, He says: My servant has spoken truth. He said: So I met Shu'bah and he narrated this to me from al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, and added in them: Whoever says them at his death, the Fire will not touch him. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it myself from 'Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah with its chain."
"5889","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Abu Salamah while he was sick, and heard weeping from behind a curtain. He said: The angels attend the dying person and say amen to what his family says; for sight follows the soul. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) closed Abu Salamah's eyes."
"5890","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, he said: When you close the eyes of the dead, say: In the name of Allah upon the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and when you carry the dead, say: In the name of Allah, and glorify Allah. And by this we take."
"5891","He informed us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from Umm al-Hasan, from Umm Salamah, that she was called to a dying person in throes, and Umm Salamah said to her: When you attend him, say: Peace be upon the messengers, and praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. And by this we take also."
"5892","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Alqamah ibn Qays, when death came to him, he said: Do not inform anyone of me, as is the practice of Jahiliyyah."
"5893","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, he said: If there is one to carry the bier, do not inform anyone, fearing it may be said: How many followed him."
"5894","From al-Thawri, from 'Asim ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: Do not inform anyone of me; sufficient for me are those who carry me to my pit."
"5895","From Hisham, the companion of al-Dastawa'i, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm, when a man dies, in informing his friend; they only disliked going around the gatherings announcing: So-and-so has died, as is the practice of Jahiliyyah."
"5896","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced the deaths of the companions of Mu'tah one by one, beginning with Zayd ibn Harithah, then Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, then he said: 'Abdullah ibn Rawahah. Then he said: The banner was taken by Khalid ibn al-Walid, and he is a sword among the swords of Allah."
"5897","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': Was a man commanded, when death attended him, to be purified by washing? He said: That is indeed good."
"5898","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': What about turning the dead person toward the qiblah when his laying on his right side is due — is that sunnah? He said: Glory be to Allah! I know of no one with sense who abandoned that for his dead; by Allah, a man's bed is even carried so he is turned with it when he cannot do it himself."
"5899","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Face the dead toward the qiblah. Sufyan said: Meaning on his right side, as he is placed in the lahd."
"5900","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about the dead person being faced toward the qiblah. He said: If you wish, face him; and if you wish, do not face him. But make the grave toward the qiblah: the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the grave of 'Umar, and the grave of Abu Bakr are toward the qiblah."
"5901","From Ibn Jurayj, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, that a man, when death came to Ibn al-Musayyab while he was lying on his back, said: Turn him. He said: Am I not upon it? Meaning he is toward the qiblah; and even if he is not facing it, because he is a Muslim."
"5902","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, that a man entered upon Ibn al-Musayyab while he was ill, lying on his back, and said: Face him toward the qiblah. Sa'id became angry and said: Am I not toward the qiblah?"
"5903","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that al-Bara' ibn Ma'rur al-Ansari, when death came to him, said to his family while in Madinah: Face me toward the Ka'bah."
"5904","From Hisham ibn Hassan, I heard it or it reached me from him, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: The angels faced Adam when death came to him, then closed his eyes."
"5905","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you attend the dying or the sick, say good, for the angels say amen to what you say."
"5906","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Abu Salamah while he was sick, and the Prophet's entering coincided with the departure of his soul; he heard weeping and said: Do not supplicate against yourselves except with good, for the angels attend the dying — or he said: the household — and say amen to their supplication. Then he said: O Allah, forgive him his sins, make his grave spacious for him, magnify his light, and illuminate his grave for him. O Allah, raise the rank of Abu Salamah among the guided, leave him behind in his offspring among those who remain, and forgive him, Lord of the worlds. Then he said: Sight follows the soul; have you not seen the staring of his eyes?"
"5907","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, he said: When the sick person sees the angel, recognition goes away — meaning recognition of people."
"5908","From Ibn Jurayj, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: My father informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you not seen a man when his sight stares? They said: Yes. He said: That is when his sight follows his soul."
"5909","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, he said: Al-Sha'bi was asked about a sword placed on the belly of the dead. He said: It is only done lest he swell; it does not harm you whether you do it or not. And he was asked about shoes being taken into the grave. He said: It is only disliked for fear of slipping. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: I do not like entering the grave with shoes; and by this we take."
"5910","From Malik, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to console the Muslims in their calamities."
"5911","From Ibn al-Taymi, from al-Hajjaj, he said: Abu 'Amrah, a shaykh of Banu Tamim, narrated to me, he said: It is said: One who consoles the afflicted is clothed with a garment of faith which will be a screen for him from the Fire."
"5912","From 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Umar, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad narrated to me that Umayyah ibn Safwan informed him that he found a sheet tied to the scabbard of Safwan or to his sword, and in it was: This is what Ibrahim asked his Lord: O Lord, what is the reward of one whose tears wet his face from fear of You? He said: My blessings. He said: What is the reward of one who consoles the grieving seeking Your Face? He said: I clothe him with garments of faith by which he attains Paradise and shields himself from the Fire. He said: What is the reward of one who fills the widow seeking Your Face? He said: And what is filling? He said: Satisfying her. I establish him in My shade and admit him to My Paradise. He said: What is the reward of one who follows the funeral seeking Your Face? He said: My angels pray over his body and escort his soul. He said: And in it was visiting the sick, but I forgot it. He said: Then Yahya ibn Ja'dah came and took it from me."
"5913","From a man, from Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Karir, from Abu 'Abdullah al-Sulami, from their scholars, he said: Whoever consoles a believer for a calamity that befell him, Allah will clothe him on the Day of Resurrection with a garment adorned by it. We said to 'Abd al-Razzaq: And how is condolence given? He said: It reached me that al-Hasan passed by the family of a dead person, stood over them, and said: May Allah magnify your reward and forgive your companion. Then he left and did not sit. We said to him: Who is consoled? He said: Every grieving one is consoled; a man may grieve for his companion and his brother more severely than his family grieves for him."
"5914","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', he said: The dead is washed an odd number: three, or five, or seven — all with water and lote, in every washing his head is washed along with the rest of his body. He said: I said: Does one suffice? He said: Yes, if it cleans him."
"5915","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The dead is washed an odd number."
"5916","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn informing us, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was washed in his shirt, and washed three times, all with water and lote; 'Ali took charge of his lower part, al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas embraced the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and al-'Abbas poured the water. He said: And 'Ali washed his lower part, and al-Fadl said to 'Ali: Relieve me, relieve me; you have cut my jugular vein; I feel something descending upon me; you have cut my jugular vein. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was washed from a well of Sa'd ibn Khuthaymah called al-Ghars at Quba'. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not wash his head except with lote. And by this we take. He said: I said to 'Abd al-Razzaq: Is one to begin with the head or with the beard? He said: The sunnah, no doubt: begin with the head, then the beard."
"5917","Humayd informed me that Ma'mar informed him, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Begin with the head, then the beard, then the right sides — meaning he was washed three times with water and lote, then with water, and that is one: every washing with water and lote, then with water, counts as one."
"5918","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': If she has two braided plaits? He said: They are undone and washed. I said: What about lote, is it indispensable? He said: You make it obligatory; as for lote, it is a purifier. I said: If no lote is found, is khitmi taken? He said: No; lote will be found."
"5919","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: The washing of the deceased is three times; whoever washes a dead person, let him cast a cloth over his face, then begin by performing ablution on him and washing his head; and when he wants to wash his private parts, let him not reach them directly, but take a rag, cast it on his hand, then insert his hand beneath the cloth and wipe his belly until impurity comes out of him."
"5920","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn 'Adi, from Ibrahim, he said: Regarding the washing of the dead: the first with plain water, performing his ablution for prayer; the second with water and lote; the third with plain water; and let him apply perfume to his places of prostration."
"5921","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I saw him washing a dead person: he cast a rag over his private part and another rag over his face, performed the ablution of prayer on him, then began with his right sides. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: Ma'mar said: And Qatadah used to say: Begin with his right sides. He said: When he wants to perform ablution on him, he removes the one on his face; as for the one on his private part, he does not move it, but puts a rag on his hand then inserts it beneath the rag. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: Ma'mar said: Ayyub said: And when they do not find lote, they wash him with ushnaan if his illness was long and much."
"5922","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: When the wasting illness of the dead is long, he is washed with ushnaan if they wish."
"5923","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from Mansur, he said: Upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a shirt, and they were called that they should not remove it."
"5924","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he said: It was disliked to wash the dead with open space between him and the sky, until there is a cover between them."
"5925","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': Is washing him naked disliked? He said: Why would he be washed naked? I said: A cloth is put on him by one over him without touching the cloth, and he is washed from beneath it. He said: That suffices him, and he is covered thereby."
"5926","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Adam was a hairy man, tall, dark-skinned, as though a lofty palm; and when death came to him, angels descended with his embalming and shroud from Paradise. When he died, they washed him with water and lote three times, put camphor in the third, shrouded him in an odd number of garments, dug a lahd for him, and prayed over him. And they said: This is the sunnah of the children of Adam after him."
"5927","From Ibn Jurayj, from Salih, the freedman of al-Taw'amah, that he heard Ibn 'Abbas say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was washed in his shirt, and 'Ali, al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, and Salih ibn Sa'dan, the freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), descended into his pit."
"5928","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: Angels descended when death came to Adam; when he saw them he recognized them, and they took his soul, washed him, shrouded him, prayed over him, buried him, while his sons looked on."
"5929","And Ma'mar said: I heard other than Thabit say: This is the sunnah of your children."
"5930","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, regarding the dead being washed, he said: A rag is placed over his face and another over his private part; when he wants to perform ablution on him, he uncovers the rag on his face and performs the ablution of prayer on him with water, then washes him with water and lote twice from his head to his feet, beginning with his right sides, and does not uncover the rag on his private part; but he wraps a rag on his hand when he wants to wash his private part, washing what is beneath the rag on his private part with water containing camphor. And the woman likewise; he begins with the beard of the dead before his head."
"5931","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I saw him wash a dead person and dry his head with live coals."
"5932","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm 'Atiyyah, she said: A daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, and he said: Wash her three times, or five, or more than that if you see fit; wash her with water and lote, and put something of camphor in the last; when you finish, inform me. She said: When we finished, we informed him, and he cast to us his waist-wrapper and said: Wrap her in it next to her body. She said: We made her head into three braids and let them down behind her — the hiqwu is a thick waist-wrapper. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Umm 'Atiyyah al-Ansariyyah, she said: A daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, then he mentioned the like of it. From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Hafsah, from Umm 'Atiyyah the like of it."
"5933","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to 'Ata': A woman's head is undone and washed with her undone for the washing in it? He said: Yes."
"5934","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed me that he heard Ibn Sirin say: There was a woman of the Ansar called Umm 'Atiyyah, among those who pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she came to Basrah hastening to a son of hers but did not reach him in time. She narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of Ma'mar's hadith. Ibn Jurayj said: I said to Ayyub: What is his saying: Wrap her in it — is she girded with it? He said: I think he only said: Wrap her in it. He said: And likewise Ibn Sirin used to command a woman to be wrapped in a shroud cloth and not girded."
"5935","Ibn Jurayj said: Ayyub said: I heard Hafsah bint Sirin say: Umm 'Atiyyah narrated to us that they made the head of the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into three braids. She said: We undid it, washed it, and made it into three braids. He said: Yes; wrap her in it, and they put it next to her body."
"5936","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: 'Ali sought from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is sought from the dead, but found nothing, and said: By my father and mother, pure living and pure dead."
"5937","He informed us, from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The dead is washed three times; if anything comes out of him after three, they wash him five times; if anything comes out of him, he is washed seven times. From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin the like of it. Hisham said: And al-Hasan said: He is washed three times; if anything comes out, what came out is washed, and he does not exceed three."
"5938","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes a dead person emerges from his sin as on the day his mother bore him. Ibn Jurayj said: And it reached me, from al-Sha'bi, the like of that, except he added in his saying: Whoever washes a dead person then is not overcome by faintness — all that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever washes a dead person emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him."
"5939","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from a man, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, he said: Whoever washes a dead person and fulfills trust in it is free of his sins as on the day his mother bore him."
"5940","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mansur ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me that he heard Yusuf, who had been a Jew then became Muslim, say: In the Torah: Whoever shrouds a dead person is like one who sponsored a child until he grew up."
"5941","From al-Thawri, from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, from Yusuf, a man who was with Ibn al-Zubayr: We find in the Book of Allah the likeness of one who shrouds the dead like one who sponsored him young until he died."
"5942","From Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', he said: Ibn 'Abbas was asked: Is washing upon one who washed a dead person? He said: No; in that case they would have deemed their companion impure. But ablution."
"5943","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he was asked: Does one who washed the dead perform ghusl? He said: If he was impure, then wash; otherwise, ablution suffices one of you."
"5944","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, he said: 'Alqamah al-Muzani informed me, he said: Four of the companions of the Tree washed your father, and they did no more than gird themselves over their clothes; when they finished, they performed ablution and prayed over him."
"5945","He said: And I heard Abu al-Sha'tha' say: Will you not fear Allah? You perform ghusl from your dead — are they impure?!"
"5946","From al-Thawri, from Jabir al-Jufi, from al-Shabi, from Alqama, from Ibn Masud, he said: If he was impure, then wash yourselves."
"5947","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Masud and Aisha: they did not consider ghusl obligatory for one who washed a dead person, and they said: If your companion was impure, then wash yourselves."
"5948","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Said b. Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn Umar: Should I take a bath on account of the dead? He said: Is he a believer? I said: I hope so. He said: Then wipe yourself on account of the believer, and do not take a bath on account of him."
"5949","From Abdullah b. Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you wash the dead, if any filth from him gets on you, take a bath; otherwise wudu suffices you."
"5950","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a bath. And we hold this view. From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from al-Harith, from Ali, the like of it."
"5951","From Mamar, from Yahya b. Abi Kathir, from a man called Ishaq, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a bath. And we hold this view."
"5952","From another besides him, from Suhayl b. Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a bath."
"5953","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a bath, and whoever lowers him into his pit, let him perform wudu."
"5954","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: The Sunna is that the one who washes the dead takes a bath."
"5955","From Mamar, from Ayyub: Ibn Sirin, when he washed a dead person, would take a bath."
"5956","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: Ibn Umar embalmed Said b. Zayd, then prayed over him and carried him, then entered the mosque to pray, and did not perform wudu. And Abd al-Razzaq holds this view."
"5957","From Malik, from Nafi: Ibn Umar embalmed Said b. Zayd and carried him, then entered the mosque to pray and did not perform wudu."
"5958","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka: the wife of Abu Bakr washed him when he died; he had bequeathed that. From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr b. Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, the like of it."
"5959","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai: the wife of Abu Bakr, Asma, washed Abu Bakr, and the wife of Abu Musa al-Ashari, Umm Abdullah, washed Abu Musa. Al-Thawri said: And we say: A man does not wash his wife, for if he wished he could marry her sister when she died. And we say: A woman washes her husband, for she is in her waiting period from him."
"5960","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard Hammad say: When a woman dies among people, the woman washes her husband, and the man his wife."
"5961","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr b. Dinar, from Abu al-Shatha, he said: A man has more right to wash his wife than her brother."
"5962","From a man of Aslam, from Dawud b. al-Husayn, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The most entitled of people to wash a woman and pray over her is her husband."
"5963","Umara b. Muhajir informed me, from Umm Jafar daughter of Muhammad, from her grandmother Asma daughter of Umays, she said: Fatima bequeathed that when she died none should wash her except me and Ali. She said: So I and Ali washed her."
"5964","From Malik, from Abdullah b. Abi Bakr: Asma daughter of Umays, the wife of Abu Bakr, washed him when he died, then she went out and asked those present of the Emigrants, and said: I am fasting, and this is a severely cold day, so is ghusl obligatory upon me? He said: No."
"5965","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid, from Abu Bakr b. Hafs b. Sad, he said: Abu Bakr commanded his wife Asma to wash him, and she was fasting, so he insisted she break her fast; she called for water before sunset and drank, and said: I will not attach sin to him today in his grave."
"5966","From Hisham b. Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: When a woman dies and they find no woman to wash her, her husband or her son washes her; and if they find a Jewess or a Christian woman, she washes her."
"5967","From Muhammad b. Rashid, he said: Abdullah b. Muhammad b. Aqil b. Abi Talib informed us: When death came to Fatima, she commanded Ali and he placed washing water for her; she bathed and purified herself, and called for her shrouding clothes; coarse clothes were brought and she wore them, and touched embalming perfume, then she commanded Ali not to uncover her when she passed away, and to wrap her as she was in her clothes. He said: I said to him: Do you know anyone who did that? He said: Yes, Kathir b. Abbas; and he wrote on the edges of his shrouds: Kathir b. Abbas testifies that there is no god but Allah."
"5968","From Mamar, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who dies in a desert land; he said: He is given tayammum, and his face is wiped with earth. Mamar said it; Hammad said it."
"5969","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: A slave girl of Safiyya daughter of Abi Ubayd fell ill with them on a journey; Nafi said to her: Your slave girl fell ill with us. Safiyya said: What if she died, what would you do with her then? I said: I do not know. She said: She is buried as she is. From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, he said: She is buried as she is. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: She is buried as she is."
"5970","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they said: She is washed with her clothes on her."
"5971","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: When a man dies among women with no man among them, he is given tayammum. And we hold this view. From Uthman b. Matar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, the like of Hammads saying: he is given tayammum. From Uthman b. Matar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, he said: he is given tayammum."
"5972","From Abu Bakr b. Ayyash, from Muhammad al-Zuhri, from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man dies among women, and a woman among men, they are given tayammum and buried, and they are in the position of one who does not find water. And we hold this view."
"5973","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me, from Aisha, she said: If Abu Amr conceals me and lowers me into my pit, he is free."
"5974","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when his daughter died, said: Let two men enter the grave who did not consort last night, meaning they did not approach women. He said: So two men entered, one of them Talha b. Abdullah; when they came out of the grave, he said: Join our predecessor Uthman. He said: They claimed she was the wife of Uthman b. Affan."
"5975","From Mamar, from Ayyub: Ibn Sirin used to perfume the dead with sukk containing musk."
"5976","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi and Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about musk for the dead, and he said: Is it not among your best perfume?"
"5977","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: he used to perfume the dead with musk, scattering powder over him."
"5978","From Mamar, from Ismail b. Umayya, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar used to apply musk to the folds and joints of the dead."
"5979","From Mamar, from Ibn Uyayna, from Ata b. al-Saib, from al-Shabi, he said: Salman had obtained some musk from Balanjar and gave it to his wife to keep; when death came to him, he said to her: Where is what I entrusted to you? She said: Here it is, and she brought it to him. He said: Sprinkle it around me, for there comes to me a creation of Allahs creation who neither eat food nor drink drink, but perceive scent."
"5980","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is musk disliked as embalming? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Then ambergris? He said: No; ambergris and musk are but the excretion of an animal."
"5981","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: Then khaluq perfume for the dead? He said: That is yellowness, and yellowness was disliked."
"5982","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar b. Ata b. Abi al-Khuwar informed me that he heard Yahya b. Yamar reporting, from a man who informed him, from Ammar b. Yasir: Ammar said: I perfumed myself with khaluq, then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he rebuked me and said: Go, O son of the mother of Ammar, and wash it off yourself. He said: So I went back and washed it off me, then returned to him, and he rebuked me again and commanded me to go back and bathe; so I did, then I returned to him, and he rebuked me and commanded me to go back and bathe, three times."
"5983","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Which embalming perfume is most beloved to you? He said: Camphor. He said: I said: Where is it placed? He said: On his soft thin-skinned parts. I said to him: On his armpit? He said: Yes, and at the bend of his legs, and in his groins, and his soft parts, and what is there, and in his mouth, nose, eyes, and ear. We said: Is camphor applied dry or moistened with water? He said: Rather, dry."
"5984","From al-Thawri, from Adi, from Ibrahim, he said: He applies perfume to his places of prostration."
"5985","From al-Thawri, he said: It reached me from Ibrahim that he disliked saffron being put into any of the perfume of the dead."
"5986","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid, from Hakim b. Jabir, he said: When al-Ashath b. Qays died, al-Hasan b. Ali said: When you wash him, do not disturb him until you bring him to me. When the washing was finished he was brought to him; he called for camphor, performed his ablution with it, and placed it on his face, hands, head, and feet. Then he said: Wrap him."
"5987","From Mamar: he saw Ayyub sprinkling powder upon the dead, his beard and chest."
"5988","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Fire is taken after the dead, meaning the censer? He said: There is no good in that. He said: Perfuming his clothes with incense? He said: Good, there is no harm in that."
"5989","From Mamar, or Ibn Jurayj, the doubt is from Abu Said, from Hisham, from his father, from Asma daughter of Abu Bakr: she said to her family: Perfume my clothes with incense when I die, then shroud me, then embalm me, and do not scatter embalming perfume upon my shroud."
"5990","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Harmala, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib bequeathed to his family not to follow him with a censer."
"5991","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Said al-Maqburi, he said: Abu Hurayra bequeathed to his family not to pitch a tent over his grave, nor to follow him with a censer, and to hasten with him."
"5992","From Malik, from Said b. Abi Said: Abu Hurayra forbade being followed with fire after his death."
"5993","From Mamar, he said: I do not know Ayyub except that he would dry the head of the dead with a censer."
"5994","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Washing the dead is odd-numbered, perfuming him with incense is odd-numbered, and his clothes are odd-numbered. And they used to say: Let not his last provision be fire following him to his grave. As many as wish may enter the grave. And he disliked the funeral being outstripped, and that a rider go ahead of the funeral, meaning he says: the fire of the censer."
"5995","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim b. al-Muhajir and Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: He used to say: The clothes are perfumed with incense before you dress him in them. And Ibrahim said: Do not perfume his body with incense, nor under his bier, nor bring anything of the censer near him, except that his clothes be perfumed before you dress him."
"5996","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Ala, he said: I was with Said b. Jubayr while he was following a funeral with a censer being taken after it; he threw it and broke it, and said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Do not imitate the People of the Book."
"5997","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman b. Musa, he said: When the deceased is perfumed with incense, begin with his head until you reach his feet, and perfume an odd number of times. I was informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that."
"5998","From Ibn al-Taymi, from al-Qasim b. al-Fadl, he said: Abu Hayya al-Thaqafi informed me, he said: Maqil b. Yasar bequeathed at his death that no flame come near, meaning a censer, and that he not be washed with hot water, and that prayer be said over him at his grave."
"5999","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid, from Hanash b. al-Mutamir: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a censer with a woman at a funeral when he was about to pray over it, and he shouted until it disappeared into the thickets of Medina."
"6000","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ali b. Husayn, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in three garments, one of them hibara. Abd al-Razzaq said: And this is what is agreed upon, and we hold this view. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Ali b. Husayn, the like of it."
"6001","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in two raytas and a red cloak."
"6002","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in two white cloaks and a red cloak."
"6003","From al-Thawri, from Jafar b. Muhammad, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in two Suhari garments and a hibara garment."
"6004","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in a Yamani suit and a shirt."
"6005","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Muhammad b. Ali b. Husayn say: It reached us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in three garments. It was said: What were they? He said: They differed about them; among them was a shirt. I said: A turban? He said: Two garments besides the shirt. Abd al-Razzaq said: And it is the shirt in which he was washed."
"6006","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in a suit and a shirt, and a niche grave was made for him. And Mamar said it, from al-Hasan."
"6007","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in three white Sahuli garments, meaning from the clothes of Sahul."
"6008","From al-Thawri, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in three white Sahul cotton garments, with no shirt or turban among them."
"6009","From Mamar, from Hisham b. Urwa, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was wrapped in a hibara garment and dried in it, then it was removed and Sahul put in its place. The hibara garment belonged to Abdullah b. Abi Bakr. He said: I shall never wear a garment that Allah removed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6010","From al-Thawri, from Abu Salama, from Aisha: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was covered in a hibara garment."
"6011","From al-Thawri, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father: Abu Bakr was shrouded in three garments, prayed over in the mosque, and buried at night."
"6012","From Mamar, from Hisham b. Urwa, from his father, he said: Abu Bakr asked Aisha: In how many garments was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shrouded? She said: In three garments. He said: And shroud me in three: this garment of mine with a tear in it, along with two other garments. And wash his garment that he used to wear. Aisha said: Shall we not buy you a new one? He said: No; the living have more need of the new; it is but for discharge. On which day did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) die? She said: On Monday. He said: What day is this? She said: Monday. He said: I hope for the night. So he died in the evening and was buried that night before morning."
"6013","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was shrouded in a Yamani suit and a shirt."
"6014","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, she said: Abu Bakr said of his two garments in which he was nursed during illness: Wash them and shroud me in them. Aisha said: Shall we not buy you a new one? He said: No; the living have more need of the new than the dead."
"6015","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman b. al-Qasim, from his father: Abu Bakr was shrouded in three garments: two dyed sheets and a garment he used to wear. And he said: The living have more need of the new; it is but for discharge, meaning pus and matter."
"6016","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: Ibn Umar used to shroud his family in five garments, among them a turban, a shirt, and three wraps. From Abdullah b. Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, similar."
"6017","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar would let an end of the turban hang over the face of the dead, then wrap it over his head from under the chin, then coil it over his head, then let the other end also hang over his face. We said to Abd al-Razzaq: And how? He said: Mamar showed us thus: he places the end of the turban letting it hang over his face, then returns what hangs over the face to the throat, then places the turban over what hangs over the face, returning it under the chin, then coils it over his head, then brings the end of the turban back over his forehead, then lets what remains of it hang over his face also."
"6018","From al-Thawri, from Asim b. Abdullah, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: Umar was shrouded in three garments: two Sahuli garments and a garment he used to wear."
"6019","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: He used to shroud in an odd number: a shirt and two wraps; he puts the shirt on him and wraps him in the two wraps, and stuffs cotton and powder. Abd al-Razzaq said: Mamar said: I know only that I saw Ayyub stuffing cotton."
"6020","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The knots are untied from the dead."
"6021","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman b. Musa, he said: The dead is shrouded in an odd number: a shirt and two wraps; the shirt is put on, one wrap spread over the other, then he is folded in them, and the shirt remains upon him."
"6022","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd b. Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah b. Amr b. al-As, he said: The dead is given a shirt, a waist-wrapper, and wrapped in the third; if there is but one garment, he is wrapped in it."
"6023","From a man of Medina, from Muhammad b. Abi Bakr, from a freedman of Abu Hurayra, he said to his family at his death: Do not turban me nor shirt me, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was neither turbaned nor shirted."
"6024","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: The dead is not given a waist-wrapper nor a cloak, but is wrapped in them wrapping."
"6025","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father: he used to shroud a man of his family in three garments, no turban among them."
"6026","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is the dead turbaned? He said: No. I said: Is cotton stuffed? He said: Yes, lest anything burst from him."
"6027","From Mamar, from Hisham b. Urwa, he said: Hamza was shrouded in a single garment. Abd al-Razzaq said: Mamar said: And it reached me that when his head was covered, his feet showed, and when we covered his feet, his head showed."
"6028","From Mamar, from Uthman al-Jazari, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Hamza was killed on the day of Uhud, and with him a man of the Ansar was killed; Safiyya daughter of Abd al-Muttalib came with two garments to shroud Hamza with them, but the Ansari had no shroud; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots between the two garments, then shrouded each of them in a garment."
"6029","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: I heard Khabbab b. al-Aratt say: We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking the Face of Allah, so our reward became due upon Allah; among us are those who passed away without consuming any of their reward, among them Musab b. Umayr, killed on the day of Uhud, leaving a cloak; when we put it over his feet his head showed, and when we put it over his head his feet showed; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded putting it over his head and placing some idhkhir over it. And among us are those whose fruit ripened and they gather it, meaning they eat it."
"6030","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Ubayd b. Umayr say: A man in that time would be shrouded in a single garment; if his head was covered his feet showed, and if his feet were covered his head showed. He said: And Abu Bakr commanded either Aisha or Asma daughter of Umays to wash two garments in which he was nursed; Aisha said: Or new clothes, or better than them? He said: The living are more entitled to that."
"6031","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What has reached you as recommended for the shroud of the dead? He said: White, at minimum. I said: I see people have hung up fine linen. He said: An innovation. And where was fine linen in that time? Is it ostentation in life and ostentation in death?"
"6032","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Samura b. Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is this white; let your living wear it, and shroud your dead in it, for it is among your best clothes."
"6033","From al-Thawri, from Habib b. Abi Thabit, from Maymun b. Abi Shabib, from Samura, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wear white clothes, for they are more pleasant and purer, and shroud your dead in them."
"6034","From Mamar, from Abdullah b. Uthman b. Khuthaym, from Said b. Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: Wear white of your clothes, for they are among your best clothes, and shroud your dead in them; and among your best kohls is ithmid, for it grows hair and clears sight. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Said b. Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6035","Yahya b. Wahb informed me, he said: I attended the funeral of Hammam b. Munabbih, and Umar b. Abd al-Hamid, a man of the descendants of Umar b. al-Khattab, attended it; he was the first who was governor for the Abbasids, and the like of him was never seen; he prayed, and upon the bier was a garment; they wanted to put it in his grave to shroud him in it, so he pulled it and said: His shroud is but what he was brought out with from his house upon him. He said: So he did not let them shroud him in it."
"6036","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Abu Salama b. Abd al-Rahman: he attended Abu Said al-Khudri while dying; Abu Said said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is raised in the clothes in which he was taken. Then Abu Said said: I have bequeathed to my family not to follow me with fire, nor to pitch a tent over my grave, and carry me upon a purple velvet cloth."
"6037","From Ibn Uyayna, from Muhammad b. Ishaq, from a man, he said: When Abu Said al-Khudri died, a red velvet cloth was placed for him; a man said: I heard him narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he saw redness and said: Verily redness has overcome you."
"6038","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim b. Ismail b. Mujammi informed me, from his aunt the daughter of Mujammi, from the daughter of Abu Said al-Khudri: he said to Ibn Umar, Anas b. Malik, and another of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Let not the sons of Abu Said overpower you over my funeral; carry me upon a Qaysarani velvet, perfume me with an ounce of incense, shroud me in the clothes in which I used to pray, remember Allah, strike no tent over me, and do not follow me with fire. And in the house was a Coptic cloth, so shroud me in it along with my clothes."
"6039","From al-Thawri, from Imran b. Abi Ata, he said: I witnessed Muhammad b. al-Hanafiyya, when Ibn Abbas died at Taif: he said takbir four times, took him from the qibla side until he entered him into his grave, and pitched a tent over him for three days."
"6040","From Yahya b. al-Ala, from Yazid b. Abdullah b. Usama, from Muhammad b. Ibrahim b. al-Harith al-Taymi, he said: The first tent pitched over the grave of any of the Muslims was over the grave of Zaynab daughter of Jahsh, and it was a hot day."
"6041","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he said: It was said: Whoever takes charge of his brother, let him make his shroud good; and it reached me that they visit one another in their shrouds."
"6042","From Ibn Jurayj and others, from Safwan b. Sulaym, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that shrouds be chosen well."
"6043","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Sila b. Zufar, he said: Hudhayfa b. al-Yaman sent me and another man to buy him a shroud; I bought him a fine red suit for three hundred dirhams; when we came to him, he said: Show me what you bought; so we showed him, and he said: Return it, and do not be extravagant in the shroud; buy me two clean white garments, for they will not be left upon me but a little, until I am dressed better than them or worse than them."
"6044","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mamar, from Abdullah b. Maysara, from al-Nazzal b. Sabra, he said: When death came to Hudhayfa, Hudhayfa said to Abu Masud al-Ansari: What part of the night is this? He said: The greater dawn. He said: Seeking refuge in Allah from the Fire; buy me two garments, and do not be extravagant upon yourselves; for if your companion is approved of, he will be dressed better than them, and if not, he will be stripped a swift stripping."
"6045","Ismail informed me, from Qays: Hudhayfa said: If your companion is approved of, he will be clothed better than it, and if not, its edges will fling him to the Day of Resurrection, meaning the Fire."
"6046","From Bishr b. Rafi, from Yahya b. Abi Kathir, from Abu Ubayda b. Abdullah b. Masud, he said: Ibn Masud said: When I die, buy me a shroud for thirty dirhams. He said: And means were ample for him. And he said: No, do not inform anyone of me except who will carry me to my pit."
"6047","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: In how many is a woman shrouded? He said: In three garments, and a garment over her in which she is wrapped. I said: And no head-cover? He said: No, but she is gathered with bands; she has a form like the form of a man."
"6048","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman b. Musa, he said: A woman is shrouded in her chemise, a head-cover, and a wrap in which she is folded."
"6049","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: A woman is shrouded in five garments: a chemise, a head-cover, and three wraps."
"6050","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: A woman is shrouded in five garments: a chemise, a head-cover, a wrap, a waist-band, and a cloak."
